  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-038',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Reyes has a strong personal belief that bloom cycles are important and worth understanding',
      'Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms',
      'Reyes, a marine biologist with years of experience, believes bloom cycles matter',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence explains the practical stakes of Reyes\'s research, connecting her bloom-cycle findings to a concrete public health concern, which strengthens the passage\'s argument for why her work matters. Cutting the final clause removes that real-world significance and leaves an incomplete justification. The third option loses all specific content about algal blooms and toxins, replacing it with vague, unsupported language. The fourth option similarly strips out the concrete stakes and adds an irrelevant biographical aside not supported elsewhere in the passage. ACT Tip: Before shortening a sentence, check whether it supplies information that gives the paragraph\'s claims real-world significance.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-020',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When the Fairview Cooperative Garden opened in 2015, its founders imagined a small plot where a dozen neighbors could grow vegetables together. [1]Ten years later, the garden have grown into a network of six lots across the neighborhood[1], supplying produce to nearly two hundred member households. Membership dues, set on a sliding scale based on income, [2]cover the cost of seeds, tools, and water without requiring outside grants[2]. Each member is expected to contribute four hours of labor per month, [3]weeding, watering, or repairing fences, whatever task the garden coordinator assigns[3]. The coordinator, a retired accountant named Devon Marsh, [4]tracks hours using a simple spreadsheet he built himself[4]. [5]Surprisingly, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first[5]. Marsh explains that many members, especially older ones, [6]found the app confusing, and so participation dropped noticeably during its brief trial[6]. What makes Fairview unusual among community gardens is its harvest-sharing model: rather than assigning each family a personal plot, [7]all produce is pooled and distributed weekly according to household size[7]. [8]This arrangement requires trust, members cannot simply claim whatever they personally planted[8]. Marsh believes the pooling system actually strengthens the community [9]because it forces neighbors to coordinate, compromise, and communicate regularly[9]. [10]Critics, meanwhile, argue that pooling discourages some members from working as hard, since individual effort is not directly rewarded[10]. The co-op\'s board addressed this concern last year by introducing a modest bonus: members who log extra hours [11]receives priority access to rare seed varieties each spring[11]. [12]Whether this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen[12]. Financially, the garden has stayed solvent without municipal funding, a fact Marsh attributes to careful budgeting and [13]a membership base that has remained loyal despite occasional disagreements[13]. [14]Looking ahead, the board hopes to add a seventh lot, this one dedicated entirely to fruit trees[14]. [15]If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Surprisingly, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'This low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first',
      'Interestingly enough, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first, which is surprising',
      'As one might expect, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "Surprisingly" accurately signals that a low-tech spreadsheet outperforming a purpose-built app is an unexpected outcome, which fits the passage\'s tone. Removing the transition loses that signal and makes the sentence feel like a flat, disconnected statement. The third option is redundant, using both "interestingly enough" and "which is surprising" to convey the same idea twice. The fourth option is a logical contradiction: the passage never suggests this outcome was expected, so "As one might expect" misrepresents the relationship between the sentences. ACT Tip: Check that a transition word matches the actual logical relationship between ideas, not just a generic sense of connection.',
  ),
