      'The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cell\'s, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow',
      'The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organisms\' cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "organism\'s" is the correct singular possessive form, referring to the cells belonging to a single dinoflagellate organism, consistent with the passage\'s earlier singular references. Removing the apostrophe entirely, as in the second option, fails to show possession at all. "Cell\'s" in the third option incorrectly adds an unnecessary possessive apostrophe to "cells," which should simply be plural, not possessive. "Organisms\'" in the fourth option incorrectly uses the plural possessive form when the sentence refers to a single organism\'s cells. ACT Tip: Determine whether the possessive noun is singular or plural before placing the apostrophe — singular possessives take \'s, plural possessives ending in s take only an apostrophe.',
  ),
