      'Her team also discovered, that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went',
      'Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'The clause "who slept poorly the week before the race" is restrictive, meaning it specifies which runners are being discussed, so it should not be set off by commas at all. NO CHANGE incorrectly inserts a comma before "recovered," wrongly separating the subject "runners who slept poorly the week before the race" from its verb. Adding a comma after "discovered" is also ungrammatical, incorrectly separating the verb from its necessary object clause. Setting the clause off with commas on both sides, as in the fourth option, incorrectly implies the clause is nonessential extra information, when it is actually essential to identifying which runners are meant. ACT Tip: A restrictive clause that identifies exactly which noun is being discussed should never be set off by commas.',
