membership base that has remained loyal despite occasional disagreements[13]. [14]Looking ahead, the board hopes to add a seventh lot, this one dedicated entirely to fruit trees[14]. [15]If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to imitate and copy',
      'If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate and reproduce',
      'If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model, that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "replicate" precisely and concisely conveys the idea of other neighborhoods copying the model, without redundancy. The second option pairs "imitate" with the near-synonym "copy," creating unnecessary repetition of the same idea. The third option similarly pairs "replicate" with "reproduce," another redundant near-synonym. The fourth option adds an unnecessary comma before the restrictive clause "that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate," incorrectly separating it from the noun it modifies. ACT Tip: When two options differ only by an added synonym paired with the original word, the version without the redundant pairing is usually correct.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-040',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s]',
    options: [
      'One fisherman kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s, which is an example of this',
      'For instance, as an example, one fisherman kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s',
      'One fisherman kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s, for example, to illustrate this point',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "for example" is placed naturally after the subject and clearly signals that this sentence illustrates the claim made in the previous sentence about fishermen\'s informal observations. The second option is redundant, pairing "for instance" with "as an example," two phrases that mean the same thing. The third option awkwardly places "for example" at the end alongside the redundant phrase "to illustrate this point." The fourth option adds an unnecessary and redundant closing clause, "which is an example of this," that restates information already clear from context. ACT Tip: A single transitional phrase is usually enough to signal that a sentence is an example — doubling up on similar phrases is a wordiness error.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-009',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'In 1911, the town of Millbrook built a clock tower above its new post office, and for nearly a century the tower\'s four faces [1]has kept perfect time[1] for shopkeepers and farmers alike. By 2019, however, corrosion in the iron gears had grown so severe that the minute hands on two faces no longer moved at all. [2]Nevertheless, the town council voted to fund a full restoration[2], hiring a horologist named Renata Ostrowski to lead the project. Ostrowski began by dismantling the movement piece by piece, [3]photographing each gear, spring, and pinion before removing it[3]. She discovered that the original brass plates, though tarnished, [4]were remarkably free of structural damage[4]. The wooden housing was another matter. Decades of leaking rainwater had rotted the beams supporting the bell, and [5]carpenters replaced them with sections of white oak, a wood chosen for its resistance to moisture[5]. Meanwhile, Ostrowski sent the gears to a foundry in Ohio, where technicians [6]re-cast the two most damaged pieces using the original 1911 molds, which the town had preserved in its archives[6]. When the parts returned, she reassembled the movement in her workshop, testing each connection [7]slow and careful[7] before installing anything back in the tower. [8]Ostrowski, along with two apprentices, spent eleven months on the project[8], a timeline that surprised even the town council, who had expected the work to take only a few weeks. [9]Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years[9]. Residents gathered in the square to hear it, and [10]many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly[10]. [11]The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved[11]. Ostrowski later said that restoring the tower taught her more about patience than any clock she had built from scratch. [12]Its now considered one of the finest examples of small-town horological preservation in the Midwest[12]. [13]Tourists visiting Millbrook often stop to watch the hands sweep past the hour, unaware of the eleven months of labor behind the mechanism[13]. [14]For Ostrowski, the tower represents something larger than a machine, it represents a promise kept to a town\'s past[14]. [15]The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years, once the movement was back in place',
      'The tower\'s bell, once the movement was back in place, rang for the first time in six years',
      'Once the movement was back in place then the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because placing the time clause "Once the movement was back in place" at the start of the sentence establishes a clear, logical sequence before naming the result, which is the clearest and most natural order for a cause-then-effect narrative moment. Moving the clause to the middle awkwardly interrupts the subject and verb of the main clause. Moving it to the end reverses the natural chronological emphasis of the passage\'s climactic moment. Adding "then" after the introductory clause is redundant, since "once" already establishes the sequence. ACT Tip: When a sentence describes a sequence of events, placing the earlier event in an introductory clause usually creates the clearest, most natural reading order.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-048',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In the hills of eastern Kentucky, a form of Appalachian English carries vocabulary and grammar patterns that trace back to eighteenth-century Scots-Irish settlers. [1]Linguist Tobias Hale have spent nine summers recording elderly speakers before their particular dialect disappears entirely[1]. [2]Hale carries a digital recorder to porches, church suppers, and county fairs, capturing conversations wherever he can[2]. [3]What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary, its distinctive grammar interests him equally[3]. Speakers might say [4]"I might could go tomorrow,"[4] stacking two modal verbs, a construction linguists call "double modals." [5]This pattern, once common across much of the rural South, has largely faded from younger speakers\' speech[5]. [6]Hale believes the decline stems less from the dialect being inherently unstable than from decades of stigma[6]. Television, radio, and formal schooling [7]have long treated Appalachian speech patterns as markers of poor education[7], discouraging younger residents from using them, even at home. [8]Hale\'s recordings, now numbering over four hundred hours, preserve not just words but the rhythm and intonation of a speech pattern rarely written down[8]. [9]He works closely with a small team of graduate students, transcribing interviews and cataloging unique vocabulary items[9]. [10]One term the team has documented is "airish," meaning chilly or breezy, a word with roots in older British dialects[10]. [11]Community reaction to the project has been mixed, some residents feel proud that their speech is being taken seriously[11]. [12]Others worry that outsiders will mock the dialect once it appears in academic papers[12]. [13]Hale addresses this concern by inviting community members to review drafts before publication and by crediting speakers by name whenever they consent[13]. [14]The archive, once complete, will be housed at a regional university, freely accessible to residents, students, and future linguists[14]. [15]For Hale, the project is less about preserving a museum piece than about honoring a living, if fading, way of speaking[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary, its distinctive grammar interests him equally]',
    options: [
      'What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary; its distinctive grammar interests him equally',
      'What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary its distinctive grammar interests him equally',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary, but its distinctive grammar, interests him equally',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'A semicolon correctly joins these two independent clauses, which are closely related in meaning but each grammatically complete on their own. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, produces a run-on sentence. The fourth option is ungrammatical, since inserting "but" and an extra comma breaks the second clause\'s structure, leaving "interests him equally" without a clear, well-formed subject. ACT Tip: When two closely related independent clauses could each stand alone as a complete sentence, a semicolon is often the cleanest way to connect them.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-024',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When the Fairview Cooperative Garden opened in 2015, its founders imagined a small plot where a dozen neighbors could grow vegetables together. [1]Ten years later, the garden have grown into a network of six lots across the neighborhood[1], supplying produce to nearly two hundred member households. Membership dues, set on a sliding scale based on income, [2]cover the cost of seeds, tools, and water without requiring outside grants[2]. Each member is expected to contribute four hours of labor per month, [3]weeding, watering, or repairing fences, whatever task the garden coordinator assigns[3]. The coordinator, a retired accountant named Devon Marsh, [4]tracks hours using a simple spreadsheet he built himself[4]. [5]Surprisingly, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first[5]. Marsh explains that many members, especially older ones, [6]found the app confusing, and so participation dropped noticeably during its brief trial[6]. What makes Fairview unusual among community gardens is its harvest-sharing model: rather than assigning each family a personal plot, [7]all produce is pooled and distributed weekly according to household size[7]. [8]This arrangement requires trust, members cannot simply claim whatever they personally planted[8]. Marsh believes the pooling system actually strengthens the community [9]because it forces neighbors to coordinate, compromise, and communicate regularly[9]. [10]Critics, meanwhile, argue that pooling discourages some members from working as hard, since individual effort is not directly rewarded[10]. The co-op\'s board addressed this concern last year by introducing a modest bonus: members who log extra hours [11]receives priority access to rare seed varieties each spring[11]. [12]Whether this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen[12]. Financially, the garden has stayed solvent without municipal funding, a fact Marsh attributes to careful budgeting and [13]a membership base that has remained loyal despite occasional disagreements[13]. [14]Looking ahead, the board hopes to add a seventh lot, this one dedicated entirely to fruit trees[14]. [15]If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: because it forces neighbors to coordinate, compromise, and communicate regularly]',
    options: [
      'because it forces neighbors into coordinating, compromise, and to communicate regularly',
      'because it forces neighbors to be coordinating, compromising, and communicative regularly',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'because it forces neighbors to coordinate, to compromise, and communicating regularly',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the three verbs "coordinate," "compromise," and "communicate" are all in matching infinitive form, satisfying the requirement for parallel structure in a list. The second option breaks parallelism by shifting to a gerund, "communicating," while the other two items remain infinitives. The third option mixes a gerund, an infinitive-less noun, and an infinitive, creating three different grammatical forms in one list. The fourth option shifts all three items into a mismatched mix of a gerund and adjectives, which is not parallel with the sentence\'s verb-based list. ACT Tip: When a sentence lists three or more items joined by "and" or "or," every item must share the same grammatical form.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-011',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1911, the town of Millbrook built a clock tower above its new post office, and for nearly a century the tower\'s four faces [1]has kept perfect time[1] for shopkeepers and farmers alike. By 2019, however, corrosion in the iron gears had grown so severe that the minute hands on two faces no longer moved at all. [2]Nevertheless, the town council voted to fund a full restoration[2], hiring a horologist named Renata Ostrowski to lead the project. Ostrowski began by dismantling the movement piece by piece, [3]photographing each gear, spring, and pinion before removing it[3]. She discovered that the original brass plates, though tarnished, [4]were remarkably free of structural damage[4]. The wooden housing was another matter. Decades of leaking rainwater had rotted the beams supporting the bell, and [5]carpenters replaced them with sections of white oak, a wood chosen for its resistance to moisture[5]. Meanwhile, Ostrowski sent the gears to a foundry in Ohio, where technicians [6]re-cast the two most damaged pieces using the original 1911 molds, which the town had preserved in its archives[6]. When the parts returned, she reassembled the movement in her workshop, testing each connection [7]slow and careful[7] before installing anything back in the tower. [8]Ostrowski, along with two apprentices, spent eleven months on the project[8], a timeline that surprised even the town council, who had expected the work to take only a few weeks. [9]Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years[9]. Residents gathered in the square to hear it, and [10]many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly[10]. [11]The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved[11]. Ostrowski later said that restoring the tower taught her more about patience than any clock she had built from scratch. [12]Its now considered one of the finest examples of small-town horological preservation in the Midwest[12]. [13]Tourists visiting Millbrook often stop to watch the hands sweep past the hour, unaware of the eleven months of labor behind the mechanism[13]. [14]For Ostrowski, the tower represents something larger than a machine, it represents a promise kept to a town\'s past[14]. [15]The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved]',
    options: [
      'The project cost far more than the council had budgeted however, the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved',
      'The project cost far more than the council had budgeted; however, the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved',
      'The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however, the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Two independent clauses joined by the conjunctive adverb "however" require a semicolon before "however" and a comma after it. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining both independent clauses with only a comma before "however." Removing the comma after "budgeted" still leaves a comma splice, since a comma alone cannot join two independent clauses. Keeping the comma before "however" without upgrading it to a semicolon repeats the same comma splice error. ACT Tip: Conjunctive adverbs like "however," "therefore," and "consequently" need a semicolon before them and a comma after when joining two independent clauses.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-033',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels, at each site',
      'measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature and, nutrient levels at each site',
      'measuring dinoflagellate density water temperature and nutrient levels at each site',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the three items in the list are properly separated by commas, and no comma is needed before "at each site" since that phrase directly modifies the entire list without a pause. Adding a comma before "at each site" incorrectly separates the modifying phrase from what it describes. Placing a comma after "and" instead of before it misapplies standard list punctuation. Removing all commas between list items fails to separate three distinct items, making the sentence confusing to parse. ACT Tip: In a list of three or more items, commas belong between each item, including before the final "and," not after it.',
  ),
