  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-061',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'For decades, marathon coaches relied mostly on intuition to decide how long a runner should rest after a race. [1]Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data[1]. [2]Her lab recruits recreational marathoners and tracks their recovery using blood markers, sleep quality, and self-reported soreness[2]. [3]Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume[3]. [4]This delay, she explains, is why runners often feel their worst on day two rather than day one[4]. [5]Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went[5]. [6]This finding surprised Chandrasekaran, who had expected race-day factors to matter more than pre-race sleep[6]. [7]Based on this data, she now recommends that runners prioritize sleep in the final week before a marathon, not just during recovery afterward[7]. [8]Another surprising result concerned nutrition, runners who ate a modest snack within thirty minutes of finishing recovered noticeably faster than those who waited an hour or more[8]. [9]Chandrasekaran suspects this window matters because the body absorbs nutrients most efficiently while circulation remains elevated from exercise[9]. [10]She cautions, however, that her sample size remains small, and larger studies are needed before the findings become general coaching advice[10]. [11]Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results[11]. [12]One coach, after implementing the new nutrition window, reported that his runners returned to normal training nearly two days faster than in previous seasons[12]. [13]Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records[13], so her team can eventually analyze a much larger dataset. [14]She hopes that within five years, recovery science will be as central to marathon training as pacing strategy already is[14]. [15]Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data]',
    options: [
      'Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, is working to replace that guesswork with data',
      'Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, working to replace that guesswork with data',
      'Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, were working to replace that guesswork with data',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The parenthetical phrase "along with a team of researchers" does not make the subject plural; the true subject remains the singular "Priya Chandrasekaran," requiring the singular verb "is." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses the plural "are." Removing the verb entirely, as in the third option, creates a sentence fragment. "Were" is both plural, matching the same agreement error as "are," and past tense, which is inconsistent with the sentence\'s present-tense description of ongoing work. ACT Tip: Phrases beginning with "along with," "together with," or "as well as" are parenthetical and do not affect subject-verb agreement.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-075',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'For decades, marathon coaches relied mostly on intuition to decide how long a runner should rest after a race. [1]Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data[1]. [2]Her lab recruits recreational marathoners and tracks their recovery using blood markers, sleep quality, and self-reported soreness[2]. [3]Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume[3]. [4]This delay, she explains, is why runners often feel their worst on day two rather than day one[4]. [5]Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went[5]. [6]This finding surprised Chandrasekaran, who had expected race-day factors to matter more than pre-race sleep[6]. [7]Based on this data, she now recommends that runners prioritize sleep in the final week before a marathon, not just during recovery afterward[7]. [8]Another surprising result concerned nutrition, runners who ate a modest snack within thirty minutes of finishing recovered noticeably faster than those who waited an hour or more[8]. [9]Chandrasekaran suspects this window matters because the body absorbs nutrients most efficiently while circulation remains elevated from exercise[9]. [10]She cautions, however, that her sample size remains small, and larger studies are needed before the findings become general coaching advice[10]. [11]Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results[11]. [12]One coach, after implementing the new nutrition window, reported that his runners returned to normal training nearly two days faster than in previous seasons[12]. [13]Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records[13], so her team can eventually analyze a much larger dataset. [14]She hopes that within five years, recovery science will be as central to marathon training as pacing strategy already is[14]. [15]Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available]',
    options: [
      'Coaches and runners alike, she says, will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available until then',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Until then, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available',
      'Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available, which is a challenge',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the sentence, as the passage\'s final line, effectively closes the essay by returning to Chandrasekaran\'s own voice with "she says" and stating a balanced, forward-looking conclusion that fits the passage\'s careful, evidence-based tone. Removing "she says" loses the direct attribution to Chandrasekaran that gives the closing sentence its authority as her own reflection. Adding "which is a challenge" is redundant, since the sentence already conveys difficulty through the phrase "balance patience with the incomplete evidence." The fourth option awkwardly moves "until then" to the end, disrupting the sentence\'s natural flow from time reference to main claim that the original order establishes. ACT Tip: For a passage\'s concluding sentence, check whether small structural or attribution details connect back to the essay\'s central voice or theme before deciding an alternative is equally effective.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-kol-063',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For decades, marathon coaches relied mostly on intuition to decide how long a runner should rest after a race. [1]Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data[1]. [2]Her lab recruits recreational marathoners and tracks their recovery using blood markers, sleep quality, and self-reported soreness[2]. [3]Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume[3]. [4]This delay, she explains, is why runners often feel their worst on day two rather than day one[4]. [5]Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went[5]. [6]This finding surprised Chandrasekaran, who had expected race-day factors to matter more than pre-race sleep[6]. [7]Based on this data, she now recommends that runners prioritize sleep in the final week before a marathon, not just during recovery afterward[7]. [8]Another surprising result concerned nutrition, runners who ate a modest snack within thirty minutes of finishing recovered noticeably faster than those who waited an hour or more[8]. [9]Chandrasekaran suspects this window matters because the body absorbs nutrients most efficiently while circulation remains elevated from exercise[9]. [10]She cautions, however, that her sample size remains small, and larger studies are needed before the findings become general coaching advice[10]. [11]Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results[11]. [12]One coach, after implementing the new nutrition window, reported that his runners returned to normal training nearly two days faster than in previous seasons[12]. [13]Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records[13], so her team can eventually analyze a much larger dataset. [14]She hopes that within five years, recovery science will be as central to marathon training as pacing strategy already is[14]. [15]Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume]',
    options: [
      'Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners presume',
      'Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners guess',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners suppose',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "assume" naturally describes a confident but mistaken belief held without direct evidence, which precisely matches runners\' mistaken expectation about when soreness peaks. "Presume" is a plausible near-synonym but often implies a belief based on some evidence or authority, which is a slightly worse fit for a common runner misconception. "Suppose" tends to suggest a more tentative or uncertain guess than the confident, widely held belief the sentence describes. "Guess" implies a lack of any real basis for the belief at all, understating how genuinely runners expect this pattern based on their subjective experience. ACT Tip: When several near-synonyms for a mental state are offered, choose the one whose degree of confidence or certainty best matches the situation described.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-010',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In 1911, the town of Millbrook built a clock tower above its new post office, and for nearly a century the tower\'s four faces [1]has kept perfect time[1] for shopkeepers and farmers alike. By 2019, however, corrosion in the iron gears had grown so severe that the minute hands on two faces no longer moved at all. [2]Nevertheless, the town council voted to fund a full restoration[2], hiring a horologist named Renata Ostrowski to lead the project. Ostrowski began by dismantling the movement piece by piece, [3]photographing each gear, spring, and pinion before removing it[3]. She discovered that the original brass plates, though tarnished, [4]were remarkably free of structural damage[4]. The wooden housing was another matter. Decades of leaking rainwater had rotted the beams supporting the bell, and [5]carpenters replaced them with sections of white oak, a wood chosen for its resistance to moisture[5]. Meanwhile, Ostrowski sent the gears to a foundry in Ohio, where technicians [6]re-cast the two most damaged pieces using the original 1911 molds, which the town had preserved in its archives[6]. When the parts returned, she reassembled the movement in her workshop, testing each connection [7]slow and careful[7] before installing anything back in the tower. [8]Ostrowski, along with two apprentices, spent eleven months on the project[8], a timeline that surprised even the town council, who had expected the work to take only a few weeks. [9]Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years[9]. Residents gathered in the square to hear it, and [10]many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly[10]. [11]The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved[11]. Ostrowski later said that restoring the tower taught her more about patience than any clock she had built from scratch. [12]Its now considered one of the finest examples of small-town horological preservation in the Midwest[12]. [13]Tourists visiting Millbrook often stop to watch the hands sweep past the hour, unaware of the eleven months of labor behind the mechanism[13]. [14]For Ostrowski, the tower represents something larger than a machine, it represents a promise kept to a town\'s past[14]. [15]The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly]',
    options: [
      'many of them, having grown up listening to the chimes wept openly',
      'many of them who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'many of them, whom had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "who" functions as the subject of the clause "had grown up listening to the chimes," and the clause is properly set off with commas on both sides as a nonrestrictive modifier describing the residents already identified as "many of them." "Whom" is incorrect because the pronoun serves as the subject of the verb "had grown up," not as an object. The "having grown up" version drops the necessary comma before "wept," creating a run-on. Removing the first comma in the last option incorrectly suggests the clause restricts which "many of them" is meant, when it is actually just added description. ACT Tip: Use "who" when the pronoun acts as the subject of its clause and "whom" only when it functions as an object.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-041',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records]',
    options: [
      'Reyes digitized his notebook; cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records',
      'Reyes digitized his notebook cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing, his dates with satellite ocean temperature records',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma before the participial phrase "cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records" correctly sets off a modifier describing how Reyes digitized the notebook. Removing the comma fuses the modifier awkwardly onto the main clause without the needed pause. Adding a comma after "cross-referencing" incorrectly splits the participle from its own object, "his dates." The semicolon in the fourth option is misused, since "cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records" is not an independent clause. ACT Tip: A participial phrase describing how or why the main action happened is typically set off from the main clause by a single comma, not a semicolon.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-026',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'When the Fairview Cooperative Garden opened in 2015, its founders imagined a small plot where a dozen neighbors could grow vegetables together. [1]Ten years later, the garden have grown into a network of six lots across the neighborhood[1], supplying produce to nearly two hundred member households. Membership dues, set on a sliding scale based on income, [2]cover the cost of seeds, tools, and water without requiring outside grants[2]. Each member is expected to contribute four hours of labor per month, [3]weeding, watering, or repairing fences, whatever task the garden coordinator assigns[3]. The coordinator, a retired accountant named Devon Marsh, [4]tracks hours using a simple spreadsheet he built himself[4]. [5]Surprisingly, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first[5]. Marsh explains that many members, especially older ones, [6]found the app confusing, and so participation dropped noticeably during its brief trial[6]. What makes Fairview unusual among community gardens is its harvest-sharing model: rather than assigning each family a personal plot, [7]all produce is pooled and distributed weekly according to household size[7]. [8]This arrangement requires trust, members cannot simply claim whatever they personally planted[8]. Marsh believes the pooling system actually strengthens the community [9]because it forces neighbors to coordinate, compromise, and communicate regularly[9]. [10]Critics, meanwhile, argue that pooling discourages some members from working as hard, since individual effort is not directly rewarded[10]. The co-op\'s board addressed this concern last year by introducing a modest bonus: members who log extra hours [11]receives priority access to rare seed varieties each spring[11]. [12]Whether this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen[12]. Financially, the garden has stayed solvent without municipal funding, a fact Marsh attributes to careful budgeting and [13]a membership base that has remained loyal despite occasional disagreements[13]. [14]Looking ahead, the board hopes to add a seventh lot, this one dedicated entirely to fruit trees[14]. [15]If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: receives priority access to rare seed varieties each spring]',
    options: [
      'receive priority access to rare seed varieties each spring',
      'receiving priority access to rare seed varieties each spring',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'received priority access to rare seed varieties each spring',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The subject "members," a plural noun, requires the plural verb "receive," not the singular "receives." NO CHANGE incorrectly pairs a plural subject with a singular verb. "Received" shifts incorrectly into past tense, which conflicts with the present-tense description of an ongoing policy. "Receiving" removes the finite verb, leaving the clause without a complete predicate. ACT Tip: Do not let a nearby singular noun, like "hours" here, distract you from identifying the true plural subject of the verb.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-015',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1911, the town of Millbrook built a clock tower above its new post office, and for nearly a century the tower\'s four faces [1]has kept perfect time[1] for shopkeepers and farmers alike. By 2019, however, corrosion in the iron gears had grown so severe that the minute hands on two faces no longer moved at all. [2]Nevertheless, the town council voted to fund a full restoration[2], hiring a horologist named Renata Ostrowski to lead the project. Ostrowski began by dismantling the movement piece by piece, [3]photographing each gear, spring, and pinion before removing it[3]. She discovered that the original brass plates, though tarnished, [4]were remarkably free of structural damage[4]. The wooden housing was another matter. Decades of leaking rainwater had rotted the beams supporting the bell, and [5]carpenters replaced them with sections of white oak, a wood chosen for its resistance to moisture[5]. Meanwhile, Ostrowski sent the gears to a foundry in Ohio, where technicians [6]re-cast the two most damaged pieces using the original 1911 molds, which the town had preserved in its archives[6]. When the parts returned, she reassembled the movement in her workshop, testing each connection [7]slow and careful[7] before installing anything back in the tower. [8]Ostrowski, along with two apprentices, spent eleven months on the project[8], a timeline that surprised even the town council, who had expected the work to take only a few weeks. [9]Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years[9]. Residents gathered in the square to hear it, and [10]many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly[10]. [11]The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved[11]. Ostrowski later said that restoring the tower taught her more about patience than any clock she had built from scratch. [12]Its now considered one of the finest examples of small-town horological preservation in the Midwest[12]. [13]Tourists visiting Millbrook often stop to watch the hands sweep past the hour, unaware of the eleven months of labor behind the mechanism[13]. [14]For Ostrowski, the tower represents something larger than a machine, it represents a promise kept to a town\'s past[14]. [15]The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The restoration, to sum up briefly and concisely, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity',
      'The restoration gave Millbrook back, in short, a piece of its identity, to conclude',
      'In conclusion, to wrap things up, the restoration gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "in short" is a concise, single transitional phrase that effectively signals a closing summary without redundancy. "To sum up briefly and concisely" is redundant, since "briefly" and "concisely" repeat the same idea and "to sum up" already implies both. "In conclusion, to wrap things up" stacks two summary phrases that mean the same thing, which is repetitive and wordy. The final option awkwardly separates the summary phrase and adds a second, redundant closing phrase at the end. ACT Tip: When multiple options express the same idea, choose the one that says it with the fewest necessary words.',
  ),
