    passageText: 'When the Fairview Cooperative Garden opened in 2015, its founders imagined a small plot where a dozen neighbors could grow vegetables together. [1]Ten years later, the garden have grown into a network of six lots across the neighborhood[1], supplying produce to nearly two hundred member households. Membership dues, set on a sliding scale based on income, [2]cover the cost of seeds, tools, and water without requiring outside grants[2]. Each member is expected to contribute four hours of labor per month, [3]weeding, watering, or repairing fences, whatever task the garden coordinator assigns[3]. The coordinator, a retired accountant named Devon Marsh, [4]tracks hours using a simple spreadsheet he built himself[4]. [5]Surprisingly, this low-tech system has proven more reliable than the app the co-op tried first[5]. Marsh explains that many members, especially older ones, [6]found the app confusing, and so participation dropped noticeably during its brief trial[6]. What makes Fairview unusual among community gardens is its harvest-sharing model: rather than assigning each family a personal plot, [7]all produce is pooled and distributed weekly according to household size[7]. [8]This arrangement requires trust, members cannot simply claim whatever they personally planted[8]. Marsh believes the pooling system actually strengthens the community [9]because it forces neighbors to coordinate, compromise, and communicate regularly[9]. [10]Critics, meanwhile, argue that pooling discourages some members from working as hard, since individual effort is not directly rewarded[10]. The co-op\'s board addressed this concern last year by introducing a modest bonus: members who log extra hours [11]receives priority access to rare seed varieties each spring[11]. [12]Whether this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen[12]. Financially, the garden has stayed solvent without municipal funding, a fact Marsh attributes to careful budgeting and [13]a membership base that has remained loyal despite occasional disagreements[13]. [14]Looking ahead, the board hopes to add a seventh lot, this one dedicated entirely to fruit trees[14]. [15]If the expansion succeeds, Fairview could become a model that other neighborhoods might choose to replicate[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Whether this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen]',
    options: [
      'This incentive resolving the underlying tension or not remains to be seen',
      'If this incentive will resolve the underlying tension or not remains to be seen',
      'Whether or not this incentive will resolve the underlying tension remains to be seen',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "whether" alone already implies the two possible outcomes, making the sentence both grammatically correct and appropriately concise. "Whether or not" is redundant, since "or not" adds nothing that "whether" does not already convey. Using "if" in place of "whether" is less precise in formal writing, where "whether" is preferred to introduce a choice between alternatives. The fourth option awkwardly uses a gerund, "resolving," in a construction that is not idiomatic English. ACT Tip: "Whether" alone is sufficient to express a choice between two possibilities; adding "or not" is almost always unnecessary.',
  ),
