  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-kol-034',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team]',
    options: [
      'Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, this pattern initially puzzled the team',
      'Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, which a pattern that initially puzzled the team',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water; a pattern that initially puzzled the team',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the appositive phrase "a pattern that initially puzzled the team" correctly renames and describes the finding just mentioned, properly set off with a single comma. The second option creates a comma splice by turning the appositive into an independent clause using "this pattern," joined with only a comma. The semicolon in the third option incorrectly treats the appositive phrase as an independent clause, which it is not. The fourth option is ungrammatical, awkwardly combining "which" and "a pattern that" in a way that does not form a coherent clause. ACT Tip: An appositive phrase renaming the preceding idea should be attached with a comma, not treated as its own independent clause.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-001',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'In 1911, the town of Millbrook built a clock tower above its new post office, and for nearly a century the tower\'s four faces [1]has kept perfect time[1] for shopkeepers and farmers alike. By 2019, however, corrosion in the iron gears had grown so severe that the minute hands on two faces no longer moved at all. [2]Nevertheless, the town council voted to fund a full restoration[2], hiring a horologist named Renata Ostrowski to lead the project. Ostrowski began by dismantling the movement piece by piece, [3]photographing each gear, spring, and pinion before removing it[3]. She discovered that the original brass plates, though tarnished, [4]were remarkably free of structural damage[4]. The wooden housing was another matter. Decades of leaking rainwater had rotted the beams supporting the bell, and [5]carpenters replaced them with sections of white oak, a wood chosen for its resistance to moisture[5]. Meanwhile, Ostrowski sent the gears to a foundry in Ohio, where technicians [6]re-cast the two most damaged pieces using the original 1911 molds, which the town had preserved in its archives[6]. When the parts returned, she reassembled the movement in her workshop, testing each connection [7]slow and careful[7] before installing anything back in the tower. [8]Ostrowski, along with two apprentices, spent eleven months on the project[8], a timeline that surprised even the town council, who had expected the work to take only a few weeks. [9]Once the movement was back in place, the tower\'s bell rang for the first time in six years[9]. Residents gathered in the square to hear it, and [10]many of them, who had grown up listening to the chimes, wept openly[10]. [11]The project cost far more than the council had budgeted, however the final result satisfied nearly everyone involved[11]. Ostrowski later said that restoring the tower taught her more about patience than any clock she had built from scratch. [12]Its now considered one of the finest examples of small-town horological preservation in the Midwest[12]. [13]Tourists visiting Millbrook often stop to watch the hands sweep past the hour, unaware of the eleven months of labor behind the mechanism[13]. [14]For Ostrowski, the tower represents something larger than a machine, it represents a promise kept to a town\'s past[14]. [15]The restoration, in short, gave Millbrook back a piece of its identity[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: has kept perfect time]',
    options: [
      'keeping perfect time',
      'have kept perfect time',
      'had kept perfect times',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The subject "faces" is plural, so the verb must agree in number: "have kept." The original NO CHANGE option pairs the plural subject with the singular verb "has," a subject-verb agreement error. "Keeping perfect time" creates a sentence fragment because it removes the finite verb the sentence needs. "Had kept perfect times" introduces both an unnecessary shift to past perfect tense and an incorrect plural "times." ACT Tip: When a subject and verb are separated by other words, trace the verb back to its actual subject before checking agreement.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-kol-071',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For decades, marathon coaches relied mostly on intuition to decide how long a runner should rest after a race. [1]Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data[1]. [2]Her lab recruits recreational marathoners and tracks their recovery using blood markers, sleep quality, and self-reported soreness[2]. [3]Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume[3]. [4]This delay, she explains, is why runners often feel their worst on day two rather than day one[4]. [5]Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went[5]. [6]This finding surprised Chandrasekaran, who had expected race-day factors to matter more than pre-race sleep[6]. [7]Based on this data, she now recommends that runners prioritize sleep in the final week before a marathon, not just during recovery afterward[7]. [8]Another surprising result concerned nutrition, runners who ate a modest snack within thirty minutes of finishing recovered noticeably faster than those who waited an hour or more[8]. [9]Chandrasekaran suspects this window matters because the body absorbs nutrients most efficiently while circulation remains elevated from exercise[9]. [10]She cautions, however, that her sample size remains small, and larger studies are needed before the findings become general coaching advice[10]. [11]Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results[11]. [12]One coach, after implementing the new nutrition window, reported that his runners returned to normal training nearly two days faster than in previous seasons[12]. [13]Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records[13], so her team can eventually analyze a much larger dataset. [14]She hopes that within five years, recovery science will be as central to marathon training as pacing strategy already is[14]. [15]Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results]',
    options: [
      'Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her introductory results',
      'Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her tentative results',
      'Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her initial results',
      'NO CHANGE',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "preliminary" precisely describes early-stage research findings that precede more definitive conclusions, matching the passage\'s earlier point about a small sample size needing larger studies. "Tentative" is a plausible near-synonym but emphasizes uncertainty about validity rather than the results\' place in an ongoing research timeline. "Introductory" typically describes material meant to introduce a topic to an audience, not early-stage scientific data, making it a poor fit here. "Initial" is close in meaning but lacks the specific connotation of results still being provisional and subject to revision that "preliminary" carries, and it is used somewhat less naturally to describe scientific findings in academic contexts. ACT Tip: When word choices are near-synonyms, check which one carries the precise connotation established by surrounding sentences, not just a similar general meaning.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-054',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In the hills of eastern Kentucky, a form of Appalachian English carries vocabulary and grammar patterns that trace back to eighteenth-century Scots-Irish settlers. [1]Linguist Tobias Hale have spent nine summers recording elderly speakers before their particular dialect disappears entirely[1]. [2]Hale carries a digital recorder to porches, church suppers, and county fairs, capturing conversations wherever he can[2]. [3]What draws him to this dialect isn\'t just its unusual vocabulary, its distinctive grammar interests him equally[3]. Speakers might say [4]"I might could go tomorrow,"[4] stacking two modal verbs, a construction linguists call "double modals." [5]This pattern, once common across much of the rural South, has largely faded from younger speakers\' speech[5]. [6]Hale believes the decline stems less from the dialect being inherently unstable than from decades of stigma[6]. Television, radio, and formal schooling [7]have long treated Appalachian speech patterns as markers of poor education[7], discouraging younger residents from using them, even at home. [8]Hale\'s recordings, now numbering over four hundred hours, preserve not just words but the rhythm and intonation of a speech pattern rarely written down[8]. [9]He works closely with a small team of graduate students, transcribing interviews and cataloging unique vocabulary items[9]. [10]One term the team has documented is "airish," meaning chilly or breezy, a word with roots in older British dialects[10]. [11]Community reaction to the project has been mixed, some residents feel proud that their speech is being taken seriously[11]. [12]Others worry that outsiders will mock the dialect once it appears in academic papers[12]. [13]Hale addresses this concern by inviting community members to review drafts before publication and by crediting speakers by name whenever they consent[13]. [14]The archive, once complete, will be housed at a regional university, freely accessible to residents, students, and future linguists[14]. [15]For Hale, the project is less about preserving a museum piece than about honoring a living, if fading, way of speaking[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: He works closely with a small team of graduate students, transcribing interviews and cataloging unique vocabulary items]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'He works closely with a small team of graduate students to transcribe interviews and catalog unique vocabulary items',
      'He works closely with a small team of graduate students, transcribing interviews and also cataloging various unique vocabulary items as well',
      'He works closely with a small team of graduate students, who help him transcribe interviews and catalog unique vocabulary items, which is important work',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the participial phrase efficiently and clearly describes the team\'s activities without unnecessary elaboration. The second option adds the vague, redundant closing phrase "which is important work," which states an opinion not directly supported elsewhere and adds no new information. The third option\'s shift to an infinitive phrase is grammatically acceptable but slightly less direct than the parallel participial structure, and it is not a clear improvement, making the original the stronger choice given no error exists. The fourth option adds redundant words "also" and "as well," which repeat the same additive meaning. ACT Tip: When NO CHANGE is grammatically correct and already concise, be cautious of alternatives that merely rephrase the same idea with added words.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-045',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Along the coast of southern Chile, fishermen have long described nights when the sea itself seems to glow. [1]This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, are caused by microscopic organisms called dinoflagellates[1], which emit light when disturbed by waves, boats, or swimming fish. Marine biologist Camila Reyes has spent the past six years [2]studying why certain bays glow brightly some nights and not at all on others[2]. Her research team collects water samples weekly, [3]measuring dinoflagellate density, water temperature, and nutrient levels at each site[3]. [4]Reyes and her colleagues discovered that blooms tend to peak after periods of unusually calm water, a pattern that initially puzzled the team[4]. [5]Calm water, they eventually realized, allows the dinoflagellates to remain near the surface, where sunlight fuels their growth[5]. When storms churn the water, the organisms are pushed deeper, [6]and their populations near the surface drop sharply within days[6]. [7]The light itself comes from a chemical reaction inside the organism\'s cells, similar to the reaction that makes fireflies glow[7]. [8]Reyes believes understanding these bloom cycles could eventually help predict harmful algal blooms, some of which release toxins dangerous to shellfish and the people who eat them[8]. [9]Local fishermen, who she interviews regularly, have provided decades of informal observations that complement her data[9]. [10]One fisherman, for example, kept a personal log of glowing nights going back to the 1980s[10]. [11]Reyes digitized his notebook, cross-referencing his dates with satellite ocean temperature records[11]. [12]The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues[12]. [13]Some scientists had long dismissed such folk observations as unreliable, however Reyes\'s project suggests otherwise[13]. [14]Her next step is to build a public forecasting tool, so that both scientists and coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science]',
    options: [
      'If the tool succeeds, it will mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'If the tool succeeded, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science',
      'If the tool succeeds, it would have marked one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'This is a real conditional describing a plausible future outcome, so the standard construction pairs a present-tense "if" clause with a future-tense main clause: "succeeds" and "will mark." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses "would," which suggests a hypothetical or unlikely scenario rather than the genuinely anticipated outcome the passage describes. "Would have marked" incorrectly shifts to a past hypothetical construction that does not match the present-tense "if" clause. Changing to "succeeded" alongside "would mark" creates a different, and here inappropriate, hypothetical conditional structure, since the passage treats the tool\'s success as a realistic future possibility, not a doubtful one. ACT Tip: Match conditional verb tenses carefully — a real, likely future condition uses present tense in the "if" clause and "will" in the main clause, not "would."',
  ),
