  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-cse-073',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For decades, marathon coaches relied mostly on intuition to decide how long a runner should rest after a race. [1]Sports scientist Priya Chandrasekaran, along with a team of researchers, are working to replace that guesswork with data[1]. [2]Her lab recruits recreational marathoners and tracks their recovery using blood markers, sleep quality, and self-reported soreness[2]. [3]Chandrasekaran found that muscle inflammation typically peaks two days after a marathon, not immediately afterward as many runners assume[3]. [4]This delay, she explains, is why runners often feel their worst on day two rather than day one[4]. [5]Her team also discovered that runners who slept poorly the week before the race, recovered more slowly regardless of how the race itself went[5]. [6]This finding surprised Chandrasekaran, who had expected race-day factors to matter more than pre-race sleep[6]. [7]Based on this data, she now recommends that runners prioritize sleep in the final week before a marathon, not just during recovery afterward[7]. [8]Another surprising result concerned nutrition, runners who ate a modest snack within thirty minutes of finishing recovered noticeably faster than those who waited an hour or more[8]. [9]Chandrasekaran suspects this window matters because the body absorbs nutrients most efficiently while circulation remains elevated from exercise[9]. [10]She cautions, however, that her sample size remains small, and larger studies are needed before the findings become general coaching advice[10]. [11]Still, several college programs have already begun adjusting their post-race protocols based on her preliminary results[11]. [12]One coach, after implementing the new nutrition window, reported that his runners returned to normal training nearly two days faster than in previous seasons[12]. [13]Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records[13], so her team can eventually analyze a much larger dataset. [14]She hopes that within five years, recovery science will be as central to marathon training as pacing strategy already is[14]. [15]Until then, she says, coaches and runners alike will have to balance patience with the incomplete evidence currently available[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records]',
    options: [
      'Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption, but urges coaches to keep detailed records',
      'Chandrasekaran welcomes this early adoption but urging coaches to keep detailed records',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Chandrasekaran welcome this early adoption but urges coaches to keep detailed records',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "Chandrasekaran welcomes" correctly uses the singular verb form for a singular subject, and no comma is needed before "but" since "but urges coaches to keep detailed records" is not an independent clause, sharing the same subject as the first verb. "Welcome," without the "-s," fails to agree with the singular subject "Chandrasekaran." Adding a comma before "but," as in the third option, is unnecessary because the second half of the sentence lacks its own subject and is not independent. "Urging," in the fourth option, removes the finite verb needed to complete the second half of the compound predicate, creating an incomplete construction. ACT Tip: A comma before a coordinating conjunction like "but" is only needed when the conjunction joins two independent clauses, not when it joins two verbs sharing the same subject.',
  ),
