    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stories the way other people collect stamps or coins, filing away who had a sick mother, whose crop had come in thin that year, which teenager had gotten into trouble and which had gotten out of it.\n\nI began to understand, slowly, that the ferry was not really about crossing water. It was about the fifteen minutes the crossing took, fifteen minutes in which people who might otherwise never speak to each other stood at the rail together and watched the same water go by. A farmer and a schoolteacher, a stranger and a regular, my grandmother and whoever needed to talk that day. She never rushed anyone off the barge, even when a line of cars waited on the far bank. She said the waiting cars could wait; the fifteen minutes belonged to whoever was standing at the rail.\n\nBy August, I could dock the ferry myself in calm water, though Odile still took over whenever the current ran fast or the wind pushed hard against the hull. I had also started noticing what she noticed: the particular way Mr. Halloran always looked at the far bank rather than at anyone on the boat, ever since his wife passed; the way the Kowalski girl, sixteen and newly licensed, gripped her steering wheel too tightly every single crossing, as though the river itself might reach up and take the car from her.\n\nToward the end of that summer, a company from the state capital came through, surveying the crossing for a bridge that would, within five years, make the ferry obsolete. Odile walked the surveyors down to the landing herself, answered their questions patiently, and even pointed out where the current ran fastest, information that would help them place the piers. I asked her, afterward, if it bothered her, helping build the thing that would put her out of work.\n\n"The bridge is coming whether I help or not," she said. "Better it goes in a place that respects the river than a place that doesn\'t." Then she added, almost as an afterthought, that a bridge would never do what the ferry did, would never make anyone stand still for fifteen minutes and look at the water and, sometimes, at each other.\n\nI think about that summer often now, decades later, whenever I\'m rushing through some transaction, some errand, some crossing of my own that I\'ve engineered to take as little time as possible. I think about the fifteen minutes Odile refused to shorten, and the particular, unhurried attention she paid to a river that could not be beaten, only listened to.',
