    questionText: 'The passage\'s final paragraph, discussing pointillism\'s legacy for later art movements, primarily functions to:',
    options: [
      'argue that pointillism was ultimately a failed artistic experiment with no lasting influence',
      'suggest that pointillism eventually became the dominant painting technique of the twentieth century',
      'provide a detailed technical explanation of how Fauvist painters applied color to canvas',
      'situate Seurat\'s technique within a broader historical arc, connecting his systematic approach to color to later movements that also treated color and form as independent subjects',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'The final paragraph explicitly states that Seurat "helped open a path toward later movements, from Fauvism to abstraction, that would treat color and form as ends in themselves," directly connecting his systematic method to a broader historical trajectory in art, which is the paragraph\'s clear function. The passage does not call pointillism a failed experiment; it describes it as "a pivotal conceptual bridge," a description of lasting significance, not failure. The passage mentions Fauvism only briefly as part of a broader historical arc and does not provide any detailed technical explanation of Fauvist methods specifically. The passage explicitly states pointillism "never truly became" a widely practiced technique, directly contradicting the claim that it became dominant in the twentieth century. ACT Tip: A passage\'s final paragraph discussing a broader legacy usually aims to connect the specific subject to a larger historical pattern, not to reverse or undercut the significance established earlier in the passage.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-031',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'When gray wolves were reintroduced to Kestrel Valley National Park in the mid-1990s, after having been eliminated from the region for nearly seventy years, wildlife biologists expected the predators to reduce the valley\'s overabundant elk population. What they did not fully anticipate was the cascade of ecological changes that would ripple outward from that single intervention, reshaping the valley\'s rivers, forests, and even its populations of songbirds and beavers in ways that would take ecologists more than two decades to fully document and understand.\n\nBefore the wolves\' return, elk in Kestrel Valley had grown accustomed to browsing along riverbanks without much concern for predation, lingering for hours at a time to graze on young willow and aspen shoots. Decades of unchecked browsing had left many streamside areas nearly stripped of young trees, since elk consistently ate saplings before they could grow tall enough to escape the animals\' reach. Ecologist Dr. Naomi Feldman, who has studied the valley since before the reintroduction, describes the pre-wolf landscape as caught in what researchers call a "browse trap": young trees were eaten faster than they could regenerate, leaving streambanks eroded, exposed, and increasingly unable to support the diverse plant and animal communities that healthy riparian zones typically sustain.\n\nThe wolves\' return changed elk behavior long before it meaningfully changed elk numbers. Within a few years, elk began avoiding the open streamside areas where they were most vulnerable to ambush, a shift researchers term the "ecology of fear," in which the mere presence of a predator alters prey behavior regardless of how many individual predators are actually nearby at any given moment. Elk increasingly retreated to denser forest cover or higher, more open terrain where they could spot approaching predators from a distance, leaving the streamside willow and aspen stands with dramatically reduced grazing pressure for the first time in generations.\n\nFreed from constant browsing, young trees along the rivers began to grow taller, and within about a decade, researchers documented aspen and willow stands reaching heights that had not been recorded in the valley since before the wolves\' original elimination. This vegetation recovery, in turn, triggered a further chain of changes. Beavers, which rely on willow for both food and construction material, began recolonizing stretches of river they had abandoned decades earlier when suitable vegetation had become too scarce to support their dam-building activities. As beaver populations grew, their dams created new ponds and wetlands, slowing river flow, raising local water tables, and creating habitat for fish, amphibians, and waterfowl that had been scarce or absent from those stretches of river for years.\n\nSongbird populations, too, responded to the vegetation recovery, though ecologists caution against assuming every observed change traces back to wolves alone. Dr. Feldman\'s research team found that certain species of warblers and flycatchers, which nest specifically in dense, tall willow thickets, increased significantly in areas where willow had recovered most dramatically, while showing little change in areas where recovery had been slower or less pronounced. This correlation, while suggestive, required years of careful comparative study across multiple sites to distinguish the wolves\' indirect influence from other possible factors, including changing precipitation patterns and separate, ongoing efforts to control invasive plant species within the park.\n\nNot every scientist agrees on how much credit the wolves themselves deserve for the valley\'s broader recovery. Some researchers argue that shifting climate patterns, which have altered precipitation and streamflow independently of any predator-prey dynamics, may account for a meaningful portion of the vegetation changes typically attributed to the wolves\' return, and that isolating the wolves\' specific contribution from these broader environmental shifts remains genuinely difficult. Dr. Feldman has generally supported the view that wolves played a central role, but she has also emphasized in her more recent publications that the story is more complicated than early, simplified accounts suggested, noting that elk population declines from increased hunting permits issued around the same period, and a series of unusually severe winters in the early 2000s, likely also contributed to reduced browsing pressure independent of wolf predation itself.\n\nWhat remains largely undisputed, however, is that the reintroduction demonstrated, in a single well-documented case, how a single species removed from or restored to an ecosystem can influence far more than the population it directly interacts with. The concept, which ecologists call a trophic cascade, had been proposed in theoretical terms decades before the Kestrel Valley reintroduction, but the valley\'s well-monitored recovery gave researchers an unusually detailed, long-term case study to test and refine those theoretical predictions against real-world data, even as debates continue over exactly how much of the observed change can be attributed to wolves alone versus the broader web of environmental factors operating alongside them.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, how long had gray wolves been eliminated from Kestrel Valley before their reintroduction in the mid-1990s?',
    options: [
