    questionText: 'According to the passage, at what age did Seurat die?',
    options: [
      'Thirty-one',
      'Thirty-nine',
      'Twenty-eight',
      'Twenty-one',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that Seurat "completed only a handful of major works before his death in 1891 at the age of thirty-one." The other ages are not mentioned anywhere in the passage and are unsupported by the text. ACT Tip: For direct biographical facts like age or date of death, locate the exact sentence stating the figure rather than estimating from other details in the passage.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-iki-019',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Integration of Knowledge and Ideas',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In 2019, the city council of a mid-sized manufacturing town in the American Midwest approved a pilot program that would, for two years, provide two hundred randomly selected low-income residents with a guaranteed monthly payment of five hundred dollars, no strings attached. The program, funded through a combination of philanthropic donations and a modest local tax increase, was designed by a team of economists to test a question that had circulated in policy circles for decades: would giving people cash directly, without requiring them to prove they were seeking work or spending the money in approved ways, actually change their behavior for better or worse?\n\nCritics of the program, before it began, offered two competing predictions. Some argued that recipients would work less, reasoning that a guaranteed income would reduce the financial pressure that pushes people into the labor market. Others worried the payments would simply be absorbed into existing spending patterns, propping up landlords and creditors without meaningfully improving recipients\' lives. Supporters countered that both predictions underestimated how much low-income households already struggle to plan more than a few weeks ahead, arguing that a predictable income floor would allow for exactly the kind of longer-term decisions, job training, moving to a better apartment, saving for a car repair, that irregular income makes nearly impossible.\n\nThe results, published three years after the program\'s launch, complicated both sets of predictions. Employment among recipients did not decline in any statistically significant way compared to a control group that received no payments; in fact, recipients were slightly more likely than the control group to have found full-time work by the end of the study period, though the difference was small enough that the research team cautioned against overstating it. What did change substantially was the composition of how people spent their time. Recipients reported working fewer hours at unstable, on-call jobs and more hours either in stable positions or in unpaid activities such as caregiving for family members and pursuing additional schooling.\n\nThe program\'s lead economist, Dr. Marcus Webb, described this shift as the study\'s most important finding, though not necessarily its most surprising one. "We expected some reshuffling," he explained in an interview after the results were published, "but what struck us was how deliberately people used the stability. They weren\'t just spending the money; they were using the certainty of it to make different choices about their time." One recipient, a hotel housekeeper who had previously worked two additional part-time jobs to cover unpredictable bills, used the guaranteed payment to drop one of those jobs and enroll in a nursing assistant certification program, a change she said she had wanted to make for years but could never afford the several months of reduced income the transition would require.\n\nNot every finding supported the program\'s supporters, however. Recipients\' self-reported measures of financial stress and anxiety improved noticeably in the first year of the program but plateaued in the second year, a pattern the research team attributed partly to the fixed nature of the payment amount. Five hundred dollars, worth a certain amount in 2019, was worth somewhat less by 2021 due to inflation, and some recipients described feeling that the initial sense of relief had partially eroded as prices for groceries, rent, and transportation rose faster than the payment itself. This finding led several members of the research team to argue, in their concluding recommendations, that any future guaranteed income program should include an inflation adjustment mechanism, a suggestion that added an unanticipated wrinkle to policy discussions that had previously focused mainly on whether such programs should exist at all rather than on their technical design.\n\nThe program also revealed unexpected effects on neighboring households who did not receive payments. Local business owners in the pilot area reported modest increases in revenue, which some economists attributed to recipients spending more within their own neighborhood rather than in nearby shopping districts, since predictable income appeared to reduce the pressure to seek out the very cheapest prices regardless of location. This spillover effect, while modest, added a further complication to simple cost-benefit assessments of the program, since it suggested that guaranteed income might generate economic benefits extending beyond the direct recipients themselves.\n\nDr. Webb was careful, in public discussions of the results, to note the limits of what a single two-year pilot in one city could establish. "We can say what happened in this particular place, with this particular group, over this particular window of time," he said. "We can\'t simply assume the same results would hold in a much larger, permanent, nationwide program, where the economic dynamics might shift in ways a small pilot cannot capture." Even so, city officials in several other municipalities have since cited the study\'s findings when proposing their own smaller-scale pilot programs, suggesting that the debate over guaranteed income, far from being settled, has entered a new and more empirically grounded phase.',
    questionText: 'Which of the following best characterizes the overall relationship between the passage\'s reported results and the predictions discussed in paragraph 2?',
    options: [
      'The results did not confirm either prediction cleanly, instead revealing a more nuanced shift in the composition of recipients\' work rather than a simple decline or absorption',
      'The results fully confirmed the prediction that payments would simply be absorbed into existing spending with no meaningful change in behavior',
      'The results were inconclusive and provided no meaningful data on recipients\' behavior at all',
      'The results fully confirmed the prediction that recipients would work less',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states the results "complicated both sets of predictions," and goes on to describe how employment did not decline while spending patterns shifted meaningfully in ways neither original prediction anticipated, such as increased schooling and caregiving, supporting this more nuanced characterization. Employment did not decline in any statistically significant way, directly contradicting the claim that the results fully confirmed the work-reduction prediction. The passage describes substantial, meaningful shifts in time use and reduced financial stress in year one, directly contradicting the claim that payments were simply absorbed with no meaningful behavioral change. The passage reports specific, detailed findings throughout, directly contradicting the claim that the results were inconclusive or lacking meaningful data. ACT Tip: When a passage explicitly states that results "complicated" earlier predictions, expect the correct answer to reflect a nuanced middle position rather than a simple confirmation of either extreme.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-cas-022',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Craft and Structure',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When Georges Seurat exhibited A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte in Paris in 1886, most visitors to the eighth Impressionist exhibition had never seen anything quite like it. From a distance, the enormous canvas depicted an unremarkable scene: Parisians relaxing along the Seine, strolling, fishing, lounging in the shade. Up close, however, the picture dissolved into thousands upon thousands of tiny, distinct dots of pure color, applied methodically across the canvas rather than blended on a palette. Seurat called his technique chromoluminarism, though history would come to know it by a simpler name: pointillism.\n\nThe technique did not emerge from artistic instinct alone. Seurat, unusually among painters of his generation, approached color with something closer to scientific rigor. He had studied the writings of the chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul, whose theory of simultaneous contrast argued that adjacent colors influence how the eye perceives each other, and he had absorbed the emerging science of optical color mixing, the idea that the human eye, when presented with tiny dots of separate colors placed close together, would blend them not on the canvas but within the retina itself. Rather than mixing orange paint directly, Seurat might place small dots of pure red and pure yellow side by side, trusting the viewer\'s eye to perform the blending at a distance, a process he believed produced a more luminous, vibrant effect than pigments physically mixed together on a palette.\n</br>This scientific ambition set Seurat apart from many of his Impressionist predecessors and contemporaries. Painters like Claude Monet and Pierre-Auguste Renoir had already broken from academic tradition by painting outdoors, capturing fleeting effects of light with loose, visible brushstrokes, but their approach remained fundamentally intuitive, guided by observation and immediate sensation rather than by systematic theory. Seurat, by contrast, treated painting almost as an experimental science, developing elaborate written notes on color relationships and planning his compositions through dozens of preparatory studies before ever touching the final canvas. Critics at the time were divided over whether this systematic approach represented an evolution of Impressionism or a betrayal of its spontaneous spirit; the critic Félix Fénéon, an early champion of Seurat\'s work, coined the term Neo-Impressionism specifically to capture this sense of both continuity with and departure from the earlier movement.\n\nThe reception of La Grande Jatte was, in its own time, deeply mixed. Some critics praised its technical audacity and its almost mathematical precision, seeing in it a serious, intellectually rigorous alternative to what they viewed as the increasingly loose and commercially compromised work of established Impressionists. Others found the painting cold and mechanical, arguing that Seurat\'s rigid application of dots drained the scene of the very spontaneity and emotional immediacy that had made Impressionism compelling in the first place. One contemporary reviewer complained that the figures in the painting looked less like Parisians enjoying a Sunday than like specimens arranged for scientific observation, their stiff, frontal poses reinforcing an impression of detachment rather than leisure.\n\nModern art historians have tended to resist this either-or framing, arguing instead that the painting\'s apparent coldness was itself a deliberate artistic choice rather than an accidental byproduct of Seurat\'s scientific method. The rigid, almost frieze-like arrangement of figures, some historians argue, deliberately echoes ancient Egyptian relief sculpture and classical composition, suggesting that Seurat was not simply failing to capture spontaneous life but was instead pursuing a kind of timeless, monumental stillness, elevating an ordinary Sunday outing to the visual register of classical art. Under this reading, the painting\'s technical rigor and its emotional restraint work together rather than in tension, both serving Seurat\'s larger ambition to create a modern art grounded in permanence rather than in the fleeting impression that had defined his predecessors\' work.\n\nSeurat\'s method, for all its influence, proved difficult to sustain, both for Seurat himself and for the artists who attempted to follow him. The laborious process of applying thousands of individual dots meant that pointillist paintings took vastly longer to complete than a typical Impressionist canvas, and Seurat completed only a handful of major works before his death in 1891 at the age of thirty-one, cutting short what many art historians believe would have been a much longer and more fully developed body of work. Paul Signac, Seurat\'s close collaborator, continued to develop and promote the technique after Seurat\'s death, gradually loosening its strict application into a looser, more expressive style sometimes called Divisionism, which retained the underlying principle of optical color mixing while abandoning some of Seurat\'s rigid, almost architectural discipline.\n\nToday, pointillism is remembered less as a widely practiced technique, which it never truly became given its demanding execution, than as a pivotal conceptual bridge between nineteenth-century Impressionism and the more abstract, theory-driven movements of the twentieth century. By treating color as a subject for systematic study rather than pure intuition, Seurat helped open a path toward later movements, from Fauvism to abstraction, that would treat color and form as ends in themselves rather than as tools for faithfully representing the visible world.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of Michel Eugène Chevreul and optical color mixing in paragraph 2 primarily serves to:',
    options: [
      'argue that Chevreul deserves more credit than Seurat for developing pointillism',
      'explain the scientific theories that informed Seurat\'s distinctive painting technique',
      'criticize Seurat for relying too heavily on scientific theory rather than artistic talent',
      'describe an experiment Chevreul personally conducted using Seurat\'s paintings',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The paragraph directly connects Chevreul\'s theory of simultaneous contrast and the concept of optical color mixing to Seurat\'s specific practice of placing separate dots of pure color for the eye to blend, functioning to explain the scientific basis behind the technique described in paragraph 1. The passage does not argue that Chevreul deserves more credit than Seurat; it presents Chevreul\'s theories as influences that Seurat applied and developed into an original artistic practice. The passage\'s tone toward Seurat\'s scientific approach is analytical and, in later paragraphs, largely favorable, not critical of his reliance on theory. The passage never describes an experiment Chevreul conducted using Seurat\'s paintings; Chevreul\'s theory of color predates and informed Seurat\'s work rather than being tested on it. ACT Tip: When a passage introduces a scientist or theorist alongside an artist, look for how the passage explicitly connects that person\'s ideas to the artist\'s actual practice.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-iki-008',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Integration of Knowledge and Ideas',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stories the way other people collect stamps or coins, filing away who had a sick mother, whose crop had come in thin that year, which teenager had gotten into trouble and which had gotten out of it.\n\nI began to understand, slowly, that the ferry was not really about crossing water. It was about the fifteen minutes the crossing took, fifteen minutes in which people who might otherwise never speak to each other stood at the rail together and watched the same water go by. A farmer and a schoolteacher, a stranger and a regular, my grandmother and whoever needed to talk that day. She never rushed anyone off the barge, even when a line of cars waited on the far bank. She said the waiting cars could wait; the fifteen minutes belonged to whoever was standing at the rail.\n\nBy August, I could dock the ferry myself in calm water, though Odile still took over whenever the current ran fast or the wind pushed hard against the hull. I had also started noticing what she noticed: the particular way Mr. Halloran always looked at the far bank rather than at anyone on the boat, ever since his wife passed; the way the Kowalski girl, sixteen and newly licensed, gripped her steering wheel too tightly every single crossing, as though the river itself might reach up and take the car from her.\n\nToward the end of that summer, a company from the state capital came through, surveying the crossing for a bridge that would, within five years, make the ferry obsolete. Odile walked the surveyors down to the landing herself, answered their questions patiently, and even pointed out where the current ran fastest, information that would help them place the piers. I asked her, afterward, if it bothered her, helping build the thing that would put her out of work.\n\n"The bridge is coming whether I help or not," she said. "Better it goes in a place that respects the river than a place that doesn\'t." Then she added, almost as an afterthought, that a bridge would never do what the ferry did, would never make anyone stand still for fifteen minutes and look at the water and, sometimes, at each other.\n\nI think about that summer often now, decades later, whenever I\'m rushing through some transaction, some errand, some crossing of my own that I\'ve engineered to take as little time as possible. I think about the fifteen minutes Odile refused to shorten, and the particular, unhurried attention she paid to a river that could not be beaten, only listened to.',
    questionText: 'The final paragraph of the passage, in which the narrator reflects on the summer "decades later," primarily functions to:',
    options: [
      'reveal that the narrator ultimately regretted spending the summer working on the ferry instead of pursuing other opportunities',
      'extend the passage\'s central theme about unhurried attention beyond the specific setting of the ferry into the narrator\'s present-day adult life',
      'suggest that the narrator eventually became a ferry operator like Odile in adulthood',
      'provide factual information about what happened to the ferry crossing after the bridge was completed',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The final paragraph explicitly connects the ferry crossing\'s fifteen minutes to the narrator\'s adult tendency toward rushed "transactions" and "errands," generalizing Odile\'s philosophy of unhurried attention beyond the original setting and into a broader reflection on the narrator\'s present life. Nothing in the passage suggests regret; the tone of the final paragraph is reflective and appreciative, not regretful. The passage does not provide any factual details about what became of the ferry crossing itself after the bridge\'s completion. The passage never states or implies that the narrator became a ferry operator; the final paragraph discusses only the narrator\'s current impatience, not a related career. ACT Tip: A passage\'s closing reflection that shifts to the present or generalizes a lesson usually functions to reinforce the passage\'s central theme, not to introduce new plot information.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-cas-002',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Craft and Structure',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stories the way other people collect stamps or coins, filing away who had a sick mother, whose crop had come in thin that year, which teenager had gotten into trouble and which had gotten out of it.\n\nI began to understand, slowly, that the ferry was not really about crossing water. It was about the fifteen minutes the crossing took, fifteen minutes in which people who might otherwise never speak to each other stood at the rail together and watched the same water go by. A farmer and a schoolteacher, a stranger and a regular, my grandmother and whoever needed to talk that day. She never rushed anyone off the barge, even when a line of cars waited on the far bank. She said the waiting cars could wait; the fifteen minutes belonged to whoever was standing at the rail.\n\nBy August, I could dock the ferry myself in calm water, though Odile still took over whenever the current ran fast or the wind pushed hard against the hull. I had also started noticing what she noticed: the particular way Mr. Halloran always looked at the far bank rather than at anyone on the boat, ever since his wife passed; the way the Kowalski girl, sixteen and newly licensed, gripped her steering wheel too tightly every single crossing, as though the river itself might reach up and take the car from her.\n\nToward the end of that summer, a company from the state capital came through, surveying the crossing for a bridge that would, within five years, make the ferry obsolete. Odile walked the surveyors down to the landing herself, answered their questions patiently, and even pointed out where the current ran fastest, information that would help them place the piers. I asked her, afterward, if it bothered her, helping build the thing that would put her out of work.\n\n"The bridge is coming whether I help or not," she said. "Better it goes in a place that respects the river than a place that doesn\'t." Then she added, almost as an afterthought, that a bridge would never do what the ferry did, would never make anyone stand still for fifteen minutes and look at the water and, sometimes, at each other.\n\nI think about that summer often now, decades later, whenever I\'m rushing through some transaction, some errand, some crossing of my own that I\'ve engineered to take as little time as possible. I think about the fifteen minutes Odile refused to shorten, and the particular, unhurried attention she paid to a river that could not be beaten, only listened to.',
    questionText: 'The statement "You don\'t beat a river" (paragraph 3) primarily functions in the passage to:',
    options: [
      'introduce a philosophy about working with, rather than against, natural forces that the narrator comes to understand more fully by the end of the passage',
      'warn the narrator about a specific danger on the river that occurred later that summer',
      'criticize other ferry operators who attempt to navigate the river more aggressively than Odile does',
      'explain why Odile decided to help the surveyors plan the new bridge',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The narrator explicitly states not understanding the line "not really" when first hearing it, then the passage traces a gradual understanding of Odile\'s approach to the river and to people, culminating in the closing reflection on "the particular, unhurried attention" required to work with things that "could not be beaten, only listened to," directly echoing this earlier line. The statement does not warn of a specific danger; no dangerous event follows it in the passage. It does not criticize other ferry operators, who are never mentioned in the passage. While Odile\'s approach does relate to her decision about the surveyors, the quoted statement itself is introduced well before that scene and functions as a broader thematic seed, not a direct explanation limited to that one decision. ACT Tip: When a quoted statement reappears in different form later in a passage, especially near the conclusion, it usually signals a central theme the passage is developing.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-010',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stories the way other people collect stamps or coins, filing away who had a sick mother, whose crop had come in thin that year, which teenager had gotten into trouble and which had gotten out of it.\n\nI began to understand, slowly, that the ferry was not really about crossing water. It was about the fifteen minutes the crossing took, fifteen minutes in which people who might otherwise never speak to each other stood at the rail together and watched the same water go by. A farmer and a schoolteacher, a stranger and a regular, my grandmother and whoever needed to talk that day. She never rushed anyone off the barge, even when a line of cars waited on the far bank. She said the waiting cars could wait; the fifteen minutes belonged to whoever was standing at the rail.\n\nBy August, I could dock the ferry myself in calm water, though Odile still took over whenever the current ran fast or the wind pushed hard against the hull. I had also started noticing what she noticed: the particular way Mr. Halloran always looked at the far bank rather than at anyone on the boat, ever since his wife passed; the way the Kowalski girl, sixteen and newly licensed, gripped her steering wheel too tightly every single crossing, as though the river itself might reach up and take the car from her.\n\nToward the end of that summer, a company from the state capital came through, surveying the crossing for a bridge that would, within five years, make the ferry obsolete. Odile walked the surveyors down to the landing herself, answered their questions patiently, and even pointed out where the current ran fastest, information that would help them place the piers. I asked her, afterward, if it bothered her, helping build the thing that would put her out of work.\n\n"The bridge is coming whether I help or not," she said. "Better it goes in a place that respects the river than a place that doesn\'t." Then she added, almost as an afterthought, that a bridge would never do what the ferry did, would never make anyone stand still for fifteen minutes and look at the water and, sometimes, at each other.\n\nI think about that summer often now, decades later, whenever I\'m rushing through some transaction, some errand, some crossing of my own that I\'ve engineered to take as little time as possible. I think about the fifteen minutes Odile refused to shorten, and the particular, unhurried attention she paid to a river that could not be beaten, only listened to.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what event led to Odile becoming the ferry operator in the first place?',
    options: [
      'She won the position in a town lottery after the previous operator retired',
      'She was hired by the state to replace an operator who had moved away',
      'She had always dreamed of running the ferry since childhood',
      'Her husband\'s death left her as the only person willing to learn the engine\'s habits',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'The passage directly states that Odile had operated the ferry "ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits." A childhood dream of running the ferry is never mentioned or implied anywhere in the passage. A town lottery is not mentioned in the passage and is an invented detail. The passage gives no indication that Odile was hired by the state or that a previous operator moved away. ACT Tip: For direct fact-based questions, locate the exact sentence in the passage that states the cause described, rather than relying on a general impression of the character\'s history.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-cas-033',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Craft and Structure',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When gray wolves were reintroduced to Kestrel Valley National Park in the mid-1990s, after having been eliminated from the region for nearly seventy years, wildlife biologists expected the predators to reduce the valley\'s overabundant elk population. What they did not fully anticipate was the cascade of ecological changes that would ripple outward from that single intervention, reshaping the valley\'s rivers, forests, and even its populations of songbirds and beavers in ways that would take ecologists more than two decades to fully document and understand.\n\nBefore the wolves\' return, elk in Kestrel Valley had grown accustomed to browsing along riverbanks without much concern for predation, lingering for hours at a time to graze on young willow and aspen shoots. Decades of unchecked browsing had left many streamside areas nearly stripped of young trees, since elk consistently ate saplings before they could grow tall enough to escape the animals\' reach. Ecologist Dr. Naomi Feldman, who has studied the valley since before the reintroduction, describes the pre-wolf landscape as caught in what researchers call a "browse trap": young trees were eaten faster than they could regenerate, leaving streambanks eroded, exposed, and increasingly unable to support the diverse plant and animal communities that healthy riparian zones typically sustain.\n\nThe wolves\' return changed elk behavior long before it meaningfully changed elk numbers. Within a few years, elk began avoiding the open streamside areas where they were most vulnerable to ambush, a shift researchers term the "ecology of fear," in which the mere presence of a predator alters prey behavior regardless of how many individual predators are actually nearby at any given moment. Elk increasingly retreated to denser forest cover or higher, more open terrain where they could spot approaching predators from a distance, leaving the streamside willow and aspen stands with dramatically reduced grazing pressure for the first time in generations.\n\nFreed from constant browsing, young trees along the rivers began to grow taller, and within about a decade, researchers documented aspen and willow stands reaching heights that had not been recorded in the valley since before the wolves\' original elimination. This vegetation recovery, in turn, triggered a further chain of changes. Beavers, which rely on willow for both food and construction material, began recolonizing stretches of river they had abandoned decades earlier when suitable vegetation had become too scarce to support their dam-building activities. As beaver populations grew, their dams created new ponds and wetlands, slowing river flow, raising local water tables, and creating habitat for fish, amphibians, and waterfowl that had been scarce or absent from those stretches of river for years.\n\nSongbird populations, too, responded to the vegetation recovery, though ecologists caution against assuming every observed change traces back to wolves alone. Dr. Feldman\'s research team found that certain species of warblers and flycatchers, which nest specifically in dense, tall willow thickets, increased significantly in areas where willow had recovered most dramatically, while showing little change in areas where recovery had been slower or less pronounced. This correlation, while suggestive, required years of careful comparative study across multiple sites to distinguish the wolves\' indirect influence from other possible factors, including changing precipitation patterns and separate, ongoing efforts to control invasive plant species within the park.\n\nNot every scientist agrees on how much credit the wolves themselves deserve for the valley\'s broader recovery. Some researchers argue that shifting climate patterns, which have altered precipitation and streamflow independently of any predator-prey dynamics, may account for a meaningful portion of the vegetation changes typically attributed to the wolves\' return, and that isolating the wolves\' specific contribution from these broader environmental shifts remains genuinely difficult. Dr. Feldman has generally supported the view that wolves played a central role, but she has also emphasized in her more recent publications that the story is more complicated than early, simplified accounts suggested, noting that elk population declines from increased hunting permits issued around the same period, and a series of unusually severe winters in the early 2000s, likely also contributed to reduced browsing pressure independent of wolf predation itself.\n\nWhat remains largely undisputed, however, is that the reintroduction demonstrated, in a single well-documented case, how a single species removed from or restored to an ecosystem can influence far more than the population it directly interacts with. The concept, which ecologists call a trophic cascade, had been proposed in theoretical terms decades before the Kestrel Valley reintroduction, but the valley\'s well-monitored recovery gave researchers an unusually detailed, long-term case study to test and refine those theoretical predictions against real-world data, even as debates continue over exactly how much of the observed change can be attributed to wolves alone versus the broader web of environmental factors operating alongside them.',
    questionText: 'As it is used in paragraph 3, the phrase "ecology of fear" most nearly refers to:',
    options: [
      'a psychological condition observed only in wolves following their reintroduction',
      'a measurable decline in the total number of elk in the valley due to direct predation',
      'a change in prey behavior caused by the mere presence of a predator, regardless of the actual number of predators nearby',
      'a decline in tourism to the park after wolves were reintroduced',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly defines the term: "the mere presence of a predator alters prey behavior regardless of how many individual predators are actually nearby at any given moment." The passage draws an explicit distinction between the wolves changing elk behavior and changing elk population numbers, noting the behavioral shift happened "long before it meaningfully changed elk numbers," directly contradicting the first option\'s focus on population decline. The term describes elk behavior, not any psychological condition in wolves themselves. Tourism is never discussed anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: When a passage places a specialized term in quotation marks, the sentence immediately surrounding it typically supplies the precise definition needed to answer a vocabulary-in-context question.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-cas-020',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Craft and Structure',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 2019, the city council of a mid-sized manufacturing town in the American Midwest approved a pilot program that would, for two years, provide two hundred randomly selected low-income residents with a guaranteed monthly payment of five hundred dollars, no strings attached. The program, funded through a combination of philanthropic donations and a modest local tax increase, was designed by a team of economists to test a question that had circulated in policy circles for decades: would giving people cash directly, without requiring them to prove they were seeking work or spending the money in approved ways, actually change their behavior for better or worse?\n\nCritics of the program, before it began, offered two competing predictions. Some argued that recipients would work less, reasoning that a guaranteed income would reduce the financial pressure that pushes people into the labor market. Others worried the payments would simply be absorbed into existing spending patterns, propping up landlords and creditors without meaningfully improving recipients\' lives. Supporters countered that both predictions underestimated how much low-income households already struggle to plan more than a few weeks ahead, arguing that a predictable income floor would allow for exactly the kind of longer-term decisions, job training, moving to a better apartment, saving for a car repair, that irregular income makes nearly impossible.\n\nThe results, published three years after the program\'s launch, complicated both sets of predictions. Employment among recipients did not decline in any statistically significant way compared to a control group that received no payments; in fact, recipients were slightly more likely than the control group to have found full-time work by the end of the study period, though the difference was small enough that the research team cautioned against overstating it. What did change substantially was the composition of how people spent their time. Recipients reported working fewer hours at unstable, on-call jobs and more hours either in stable positions or in unpaid activities such as caregiving for family members and pursuing additional schooling.\n\nThe program\'s lead economist, Dr. Marcus Webb, described this shift as the study\'s most important finding, though not necessarily its most surprising one. "We expected some reshuffling," he explained in an interview after the results were published, "but what struck us was how deliberately people used the stability. They weren\'t just spending the money; they were using the certainty of it to make different choices about their time." One recipient, a hotel housekeeper who had previously worked two additional part-time jobs to cover unpredictable bills, used the guaranteed payment to drop one of those jobs and enroll in a nursing assistant certification program, a change she said she had wanted to make for years but could never afford the several months of reduced income the transition would require.\n\nNot every finding supported the program\'s supporters, however. Recipients\' self-reported measures of financial stress and anxiety improved noticeably in the first year of the program but plateaued in the second year, a pattern the research team attributed partly to the fixed nature of the payment amount. Five hundred dollars, worth a certain amount in 2019, was worth somewhat less by 2021 due to inflation, and some recipients described feeling that the initial sense of relief had partially eroded as prices for groceries, rent, and transportation rose faster than the payment itself. This finding led several members of the research team to argue, in their concluding recommendations, that any future guaranteed income program should include an inflation adjustment mechanism, a suggestion that added an unanticipated wrinkle to policy discussions that had previously focused mainly on whether such programs should exist at all rather than on their technical design.\n\nThe program also revealed unexpected effects on neighboring households who did not receive payments. Local business owners in the pilot area reported modest increases in revenue, which some economists attributed to recipients spending more within their own neighborhood rather than in nearby shopping districts, since predictable income appeared to reduce the pressure to seek out the very cheapest prices regardless of location. This spillover effect, while modest, added a further complication to simple cost-benefit assessments of the program, since it suggested that guaranteed income might generate economic benefits extending beyond the direct recipients themselves.\n\nDr. Webb was careful, in public discussions of the results, to note the limits of what a single two-year pilot in one city could establish. "We can say what happened in this particular place, with this particular group, over this particular window of time," he said. "We can\'t simply assume the same results would hold in a much larger, permanent, nationwide program, where the economic dynamics might shift in ways a small pilot cannot capture." Even so, city officials in several other municipalities have since cited the study\'s findings when proposing their own smaller-scale pilot programs, suggesting that the debate over guaranteed income, far from being settled, has entered a new and more empirically grounded phase.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s inclusion of the hotel housekeeper\'s story in paragraph 4 primarily functions to:',
    options: [
      'demonstrate that the program was too small in scale to produce any measurable statistical results',
      'criticize the housekeeping industry for providing inadequate wages to its workers',
      'provide a concrete, specific illustration of the general pattern Dr. Webb describes about recipients using payment stability to make deliberate life changes',
      'prove that all recipients used their payments to pursue additional education or job certifications',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The housekeeper\'s example directly follows and illustrates Webb\'s general claim that recipients "were using the certainty of it to make different choices about their time," functioning as a specific, concrete case supporting that broader pattern. The passage never claims all recipients pursued education or certifications; the housekeeper is presented as one specific example, not a universal pattern. The passage does not criticize the housekeeping industry\'s wages; it focuses on how the guaranteed payment enabled a career transition, not on critiquing her prior employer. The passage reports statistically significant findings about employment and measurable shifts in time use, directly contradicting the idea that the program was too small to produce measurable results. ACT Tip: A specific individual anecdote that follows a general claim in a passage is typically included to illustrate that claim concretely, not to establish a separate or contradictory point.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-023',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When Georges Seurat exhibited A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte in Paris in 1886, most visitors to the eighth Impressionist exhibition had never seen anything quite like it. From a distance, the enormous canvas depicted an unremarkable scene: Parisians relaxing along the Seine, strolling, fishing, lounging in the shade. Up close, however, the picture dissolved into thousands upon thousands of tiny, distinct dots of pure color, applied methodically across the canvas rather than blended on a palette. Seurat called his technique chromoluminarism, though history would come to know it by a simpler name: pointillism.\n\nThe technique did not emerge from artistic instinct alone. Seurat, unusually among painters of his generation, approached color with something closer to scientific rigor. He had studied the writings of the chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul, whose theory of simultaneous contrast argued that adjacent colors influence how the eye perceives each other, and he had absorbed the emerging science of optical color mixing, the idea that the human eye, when presented with tiny dots of separate colors placed close together, would blend them not on the canvas but within the retina itself. Rather than mixing orange paint directly, Seurat might place small dots of pure red and pure yellow side by side, trusting the viewer\'s eye to perform the blending at a distance, a process he believed produced a more luminous, vibrant effect than pigments physically mixed together on a palette.\n</br>This scientific ambition set Seurat apart from many of his Impressionist predecessors and contemporaries. Painters like Claude Monet and Pierre-Auguste Renoir had already broken from academic tradition by painting outdoors, capturing fleeting effects of light with loose, visible brushstrokes, but their approach remained fundamentally intuitive, guided by observation and immediate sensation rather than by systematic theory. Seurat, by contrast, treated painting almost as an experimental science, developing elaborate written notes on color relationships and planning his compositions through dozens of preparatory studies before ever touching the final canvas. Critics at the time were divided over whether this systematic approach represented an evolution of Impressionism or a betrayal of its spontaneous spirit; the critic Félix Fénéon, an early champion of Seurat\'s work, coined the term Neo-Impressionism specifically to capture this sense of both continuity with and departure from the earlier movement.\n\nThe reception of La Grande Jatte was, in its own time, deeply mixed. Some critics praised its technical audacity and its almost mathematical precision, seeing in it a serious, intellectually rigorous alternative to what they viewed as the increasingly loose and commercially compromised work of established Impressionists. Others found the painting cold and mechanical, arguing that Seurat\'s rigid application of dots drained the scene of the very spontaneity and emotional immediacy that had made Impressionism compelling in the first place. One contemporary reviewer complained that the figures in the painting looked less like Parisians enjoying a Sunday than like specimens arranged for scientific observation, their stiff, frontal poses reinforcing an impression of detachment rather than leisure.\n\nModern art historians have tended to resist this either-or framing, arguing instead that the painting\'s apparent coldness was itself a deliberate artistic choice rather than an accidental byproduct of Seurat\'s scientific method. The rigid, almost frieze-like arrangement of figures, some historians argue, deliberately echoes ancient Egyptian relief sculpture and classical composition, suggesting that Seurat was not simply failing to capture spontaneous life but was instead pursuing a kind of timeless, monumental stillness, elevating an ordinary Sunday outing to the visual register of classical art. Under this reading, the painting\'s technical rigor and its emotional restraint work together rather than in tension, both serving Seurat\'s larger ambition to create a modern art grounded in permanence rather than in the fleeting impression that had defined his predecessors\' work.\n\nSeurat\'s method, for all its influence, proved difficult to sustain, both for Seurat himself and for the artists who attempted to follow him. The laborious process of applying thousands of individual dots meant that pointillist paintings took vastly longer to complete than a typical Impressionist canvas, and Seurat completed only a handful of major works before his death in 1891 at the age of thirty-one, cutting short what many art historians believe would have been a much longer and more fully developed body of work. Paul Signac, Seurat\'s close collaborator, continued to develop and promote the technique after Seurat\'s death, gradually loosening its strict application into a looser, more expressive style sometimes called Divisionism, which retained the underlying principle of optical color mixing while abandoning some of Seurat\'s rigid, almost architectural discipline.\n\nToday, pointillism is remembered less as a widely practiced technique, which it never truly became given its demanding execution, than as a pivotal conceptual bridge between nineteenth-century Impressionism and the more abstract, theory-driven movements of the twentieth century. By treating color as a subject for systematic study rather than pure intuition, Seurat helped open a path toward later movements, from Fauvism to abstraction, that would treat color and form as ends in themselves rather than as tools for faithfully representing the visible world.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, how did Seurat\'s approach to painting differ from that of Monet and Renoir?',
    options: [
      'Seurat rejected the use of color entirely, while Monet and Renoir emphasized vibrant color above all else',
      'Seurat painted primarily indoors, while Monet and Renoir painted primarily outdoors',
      'Seurat\'s approach was systematic and theory-driven, while Monet and Renoir\'s approach remained largely intuitive and guided by immediate observation',
      'Seurat painted much faster than Monet and Renoir, completing dozens of major works in a short career',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The passage states that Monet and Renoir\'s approach "remained fundamentally intuitive, guided by observation and immediate sensation rather than by systematic theory," while Seurat "treated painting almost as an experimental science," directly supporting this contrast. The passage does not claim Seurat painted indoors while the others painted outdoors; it discusses Monet and Renoir painting outdoors but does not address where Seurat painted. The passage never suggests Seurat rejected color; his technique was fundamentally about the careful, scientific application of color. The passage states the opposite regarding speed: Seurat\'s laborious dot technique meant his paintings "took vastly longer to complete," and he finished only a handful of major works, not dozens. ACT Tip: When a passage sets up a contrast between two approaches, locate the specific descriptive language used for each side rather than assuming a difference the passage does not actually state.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-iki-015',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Integration of Knowledge and Ideas',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In 2019, the city council of a mid-sized manufacturing town in the American Midwest approved a pilot program that would, for two years, provide two hundred randomly selected low-income residents with a guaranteed monthly payment of five hundred dollars, no strings attached. The program, funded through a combination of philanthropic donations and a modest local tax increase, was designed by a team of economists to test a question that had circulated in policy circles for decades: would giving people cash directly, without requiring them to prove they were seeking work or spending the money in approved ways, actually change their behavior for better or worse?\n\nCritics of the program, before it began, offered two competing predictions. Some argued that recipients would work less, reasoning that a guaranteed income would reduce the financial pressure that pushes people into the labor market. Others worried the payments would simply be absorbed into existing spending patterns, propping up landlords and creditors without meaningfully improving recipients\' lives. Supporters countered that both predictions underestimated how much low-income households already struggle to plan more than a few weeks ahead, arguing that a predictable income floor would allow for exactly the kind of longer-term decisions, job training, moving to a better apartment, saving for a car repair, that irregular income makes nearly impossible.\n\nThe results, published three years after the program\'s launch, complicated both sets of predictions. Employment among recipients did not decline in any statistically significant way compared to a control group that received no payments; in fact, recipients were slightly more likely than the control group to have found full-time work by the end of the study period, though the difference was small enough that the research team cautioned against overstating it. What did change substantially was the composition of how people spent their time. Recipients reported working fewer hours at unstable, on-call jobs and more hours either in stable positions or in unpaid activities such as caregiving for family members and pursuing additional schooling.\n\nThe program\'s lead economist, Dr. Marcus Webb, described this shift as the study\'s most important finding, though not necessarily its most surprising one. "We expected some reshuffling," he explained in an interview after the results were published, "but what struck us was how deliberately people used the stability. They weren\'t just spending the money; they were using the certainty of it to make different choices about their time." One recipient, a hotel housekeeper who had previously worked two additional part-time jobs to cover unpredictable bills, used the guaranteed payment to drop one of those jobs and enroll in a nursing assistant certification program, a change she said she had wanted to make for years but could never afford the several months of reduced income the transition would require.\n\nNot every finding supported the program\'s supporters, however. Recipients\' self-reported measures of financial stress and anxiety improved noticeably in the first year of the program but plateaued in the second year, a pattern the research team attributed partly to the fixed nature of the payment amount. Five hundred dollars, worth a certain amount in 2019, was worth somewhat less by 2021 due to inflation, and some recipients described feeling that the initial sense of relief had partially eroded as prices for groceries, rent, and transportation rose faster than the payment itself. This finding led several members of the research team to argue, in their concluding recommendations, that any future guaranteed income program should include an inflation adjustment mechanism, a suggestion that added an unanticipated wrinkle to policy discussions that had previously focused mainly on whether such programs should exist at all rather than on their technical design.\n\nThe program also revealed unexpected effects on neighboring households who did not receive payments. Local business owners in the pilot area reported modest increases in revenue, which some economists attributed to recipients spending more within their own neighborhood rather than in nearby shopping districts, since predictable income appeared to reduce the pressure to seek out the very cheapest prices regardless of location. This spillover effect, while modest, added a further complication to simple cost-benefit assessments of the program, since it suggested that guaranteed income might generate economic benefits extending beyond the direct recipients themselves.\n\nDr. Webb was careful, in public discussions of the results, to note the limits of what a single two-year pilot in one city could establish. "We can say what happened in this particular place, with this particular group, over this particular window of time," he said. "We can\'t simply assume the same results would hold in a much larger, permanent, nationwide program, where the economic dynamics might shift in ways a small pilot cannot capture." Even so, city officials in several other municipalities have since cited the study\'s findings when proposing their own smaller-scale pilot programs, suggesting that the debate over guaranteed income, far from being settled, has entered a new and more empirically grounded phase.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of the plateau in recipients\' self-reported financial stress (paragraph 5) is best understood as evidence for which broader point made later in the passage?',
    options: [
      'That the program should be discontinued because it failed to reduce financial stress at all',
      'That recipients had become dependent on the payments and were unable to manage their own finances',
      'That future guaranteed income programs might benefit from an inflation adjustment mechanism, a design consideration Dr. Webb\'s team recommended',
      'That the program\'s spillover effects on local businesses were ultimately negative',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The passage directly connects the plateau in reported financial stress to the payment\'s fixed amount losing value to inflation, and explicitly states this pattern "led several members of the research team to argue...that any future guaranteed income program should include an inflation adjustment mechanism," tying the stress plateau directly to this specific policy recommendation. The passage does not suggest the program should be discontinued; stress improved noticeably in year one and only plateaued, rather than reversed, in year two. The passage attributes the plateau to inflation eroding the payment\'s value, not to recipients mismanaging their finances, which is never suggested anywhere in the passage. The spillover effects on local businesses are described as positive, with increased local revenue, not negative, making this option contradict the passage directly. ACT Tip: When a passage presents a specific data pattern and later ties it to a stated recommendation, connect the two by looking for the passage\'s own explicit causal language linking them.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-037',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When gray wolves were reintroduced to Kestrel Valley National Park in the mid-1990s, after having been eliminated from the region for nearly seventy years, wildlife biologists expected the predators to reduce the valley\'s overabundant elk population. What they did not fully anticipate was the cascade of ecological changes that would ripple outward from that single intervention, reshaping the valley\'s rivers, forests, and even its populations of songbirds and beavers in ways that would take ecologists more than two decades to fully document and understand.\n\nBefore the wolves\' return, elk in Kestrel Valley had grown accustomed to browsing along riverbanks without much concern for predation, lingering for hours at a time to graze on young willow and aspen shoots. Decades of unchecked browsing had left many streamside areas nearly stripped of young trees, since elk consistently ate saplings before they could grow tall enough to escape the animals\' reach. Ecologist Dr. Naomi Feldman, who has studied the valley since before the reintroduction, describes the pre-wolf landscape as caught in what researchers call a "browse trap": young trees were eaten faster than they could regenerate, leaving streambanks eroded, exposed, and increasingly unable to support the diverse plant and animal communities that healthy riparian zones typically sustain.\n\nThe wolves\' return changed elk behavior long before it meaningfully changed elk numbers. Within a few years, elk began avoiding the open streamside areas where they were most vulnerable to ambush, a shift researchers term the "ecology of fear," in which the mere presence of a predator alters prey behavior regardless of how many individual predators are actually nearby at any given moment. Elk increasingly retreated to denser forest cover or higher, more open terrain where they could spot approaching predators from a distance, leaving the streamside willow and aspen stands with dramatically reduced grazing pressure for the first time in generations.\n\nFreed from constant browsing, young trees along the rivers began to grow taller, and within about a decade, researchers documented aspen and willow stands reaching heights that had not been recorded in the valley since before the wolves\' original elimination. This vegetation recovery, in turn, triggered a further chain of changes. Beavers, which rely on willow for both food and construction material, began recolonizing stretches of river they had abandoned decades earlier when suitable vegetation had become too scarce to support their dam-building activities. As beaver populations grew, their dams created new ponds and wetlands, slowing river flow, raising local water tables, and creating habitat for fish, amphibians, and waterfowl that had been scarce or absent from those stretches of river for years.\n\nSongbird populations, too, responded to the vegetation recovery, though ecologists caution against assuming every observed change traces back to wolves alone. Dr. Feldman\'s research team found that certain species of warblers and flycatchers, which nest specifically in dense, tall willow thickets, increased significantly in areas where willow had recovered most dramatically, while showing little change in areas where recovery had been slower or less pronounced. This correlation, while suggestive, required years of careful comparative study across multiple sites to distinguish the wolves\' indirect influence from other possible factors, including changing precipitation patterns and separate, ongoing efforts to control invasive plant species within the park.\n\nNot every scientist agrees on how much credit the wolves themselves deserve for the valley\'s broader recovery. Some researchers argue that shifting climate patterns, which have altered precipitation and streamflow independently of any predator-prey dynamics, may account for a meaningful portion of the vegetation changes typically attributed to the wolves\' return, and that isolating the wolves\' specific contribution from these broader environmental shifts remains genuinely difficult. Dr. Feldman has generally supported the view that wolves played a central role, but she has also emphasized in her more recent publications that the story is more complicated than early, simplified accounts suggested, noting that elk population declines from increased hunting permits issued around the same period, and a series of unusually severe winters in the early 2000s, likely also contributed to reduced browsing pressure independent of wolf predation itself.\n\nWhat remains largely undisputed, however, is that the reintroduction demonstrated, in a single well-documented case, how a single species removed from or restored to an ecosystem can influence far more than the population it directly interacts with. The concept, which ecologists call a trophic cascade, had been proposed in theoretical terms decades before the Kestrel Valley reintroduction, but the valley\'s well-monitored recovery gave researchers an unusually detailed, long-term case study to test and refine those theoretical predictions against real-world data, even as debates continue over exactly how much of the observed change can be attributed to wolves alone versus the broader web of environmental factors operating alongside them.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what factors, besides wolf predation, does Dr. Feldman suggest may have also contributed to reduced elk browsing pressure?',
    options: [
      'Increased hunting permits and a series of unusually severe winters in the early 2000s',
      'Competition from a newly introduced species of deer and habitat destruction from wildfires',
      'Decreased tourism and reduced funding for park conservation efforts',
      'A disease outbreak among the elk population and reduced water availability',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage states Dr. Feldman noted "elk population declines from increased hunting permits issued around the same period, and a series of unusually severe winters in the early 2000s, likely also contributed to reduced browsing pressure independent of wolf predation itself." A disease outbreak and reduced water availability are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. A newly introduced deer species and wildfire habitat destruction are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. Tourism funding and conservation budgets are not discussed anywhere in the passage in connection with elk population changes. ACT Tip: When a passage lists multiple contributing factors attributed to a specific researcher, locate that researcher\'s specific statement rather than assuming any plausible-sounding ecological factor applies.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-001',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stories the way other people collect stamps or coins, filing away who had a sick mother, whose crop had come in thin that year, which teenager had gotten into trouble and which had gotten out of it.\n\nI began to understand, slowly, that the ferry was not really about crossing water. It was about the fifteen minutes the crossing took, fifteen minutes in which people who might otherwise never speak to each other stood at the rail together and watched the same water go by. A farmer and a schoolteacher, a stranger and a regular, my grandmother and whoever needed to talk that day. She never rushed anyone off the barge, even when a line of cars waited on the far bank. She said the waiting cars could wait; the fifteen minutes belonged to whoever was standing at the rail.\n\nBy August, I could dock the ferry myself in calm water, though Odile still took over whenever the current ran fast or the wind pushed hard against the hull. I had also started noticing what she noticed: the particular way Mr. Halloran always looked at the far bank rather than at anyone on the boat, ever since his wife passed; the way the Kowalski girl, sixteen and newly licensed, gripped her steering wheel too tightly every single crossing, as though the river itself might reach up and take the car from her.\n\nToward the end of that summer, a company from the state capital came through, surveying the crossing for a bridge that would, within five years, make the ferry obsolete. Odile walked the surveyors down to the landing herself, answered their questions patiently, and even pointed out where the current ran fastest, information that would help them place the piers. I asked her, afterward, if it bothered her, helping build the thing that would put her out of work.\n\n"The bridge is coming whether I help or not," she said. "Better it goes in a place that respects the river than a place that doesn\'t." Then she added, almost as an afterthought, that a bridge would never do what the ferry did, would never make anyone stand still for fifteen minutes and look at the water and, sometimes, at each other.\n\nI think about that summer often now, decades later, whenever I\'m rushing through some transaction, some errand, some crossing of my own that I\'ve engineered to take as little time as possible. I think about the fifteen minutes Odile refused to shorten, and the particular, unhurried attention she paid to a river that could not be beaten, only listened to.',
    questionText: 'Based on the passage, the narrator\'s initial expectation about working on the ferry was that the job would be:',
    options: [
      'tedious',
      'dangerous',
      'profitable',
      'confusing',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage directly states that the narrator "had expected the job to be tedious" before being surprised by fascination with the river\'s moods. "Dangerous" is not supported anywhere in the passage; the narrator expresses no fear, only boredom. "Profitable" is never discussed as an expectation the narrator held. "Confusing" describes the narrator\'s later reaction to Odile\'s river philosophy, not the initial expectation about the job itself. ACT Tip: When a question asks about an early impression or expectation, look for the specific sentence stating it directly rather than inferring from the narrator\'s later feelings.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-035',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
