\nThe program also revealed unexpected effects on neighboring households who did not receive payments. Local business owners in the pilot area reported modest increases in revenue, which some economists attributed to recipients spending more within their own neighborhood rather than in nearby shopping districts, since predictable income appeared to reduce the pressure to seek out the very cheapest prices regardless of location. This spillover effect, while modest, added a further complication to simple cost-benefit assessments of the program, since it suggested that guaranteed income might generate economic benefits extending beyond the direct recipients themselves.\n\nDr. Webb was careful, in public discussions of the results, to note the limits of what a single two-year pilot in one city could establish. "We can say what happened in this particular place, with this particular group, over this particular window of time," he said. "We can\'t simply assume the same results would hold in a much larger, permanent, nationwide program, where the economic dynamics might shift in ways a small pilot cannot capture." Even so, city officials in several other municipalities have since cited the study\'s findings when proposing their own smaller-scale pilot programs, suggesting that the debate over guaranteed income, far from being settled, has entered a new and more empirically grounded phase.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of critics\' predictions in paragraph 2 primarily serves to:',
    options: [
      'provide background on the history of guaranteed income programs in other countries',
      'prove that the critics were entirely correct about the program\'s likely failure',
      'establish competing expectations against which the passage later evaluates the program\'s actual results',
      'argue that the program\'s supporters had no legitimate basis for their optimism',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The paragraph lays out two distinct critical predictions, reduced work incentive and simple absorption into existing spending, which the passage explicitly returns to and complicates in paragraph 3, stating the results "complicated both sets of predictions," showing that paragraph 2\'s purpose is to set up this later comparison. The passage does not prove the critics correct; in fact it shows employment did not decline as some critics predicted. The passage does not argue supporters lacked a legitimate basis; it instead reports evidence partially supporting supporters\' claims about long-term planning. The paragraph discusses predictions specific to this program, not a history of guaranteed income programs in other countries, which the passage never discusses. ACT Tip: When a passage introduces competing predictions or hypotheses early on, expect the passage to return to them later to show which predictions were and were not supported by the evidence.',
  ),
