    questionText: 'As it is used in paragraph 4, the word "deliberately" most nearly means:',
    options: [
      'accidentally',
      'intentionally and thoughtfully',
      'slowly and hesitantly',
      'secretly',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'In context, Dr. Webb describes recipients as "deliberately" using the stability of the payment to make different choices about their time, meaning they did so purposefully and with consideration, as illustrated by the housekeeper\'s decision to pursue certification. "Accidentally" is essentially the opposite of the intended meaning, since the passage emphasizes intentional decision-making, not chance. "Slowly and hesitantly" describes a pace or manner of action not supported by the passage\'s description of purposeful choices. "Secretly" is unrelated to the sentence\'s meaning, since nothing in the passage suggests recipients were concealing their decisions. ACT Tip: When a vocabulary-in-context question follows a sentence with a supporting example, check whether the example clarifies which sense of the word is intended.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-reading-kid-007',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'The summer I turned fourteen, my grandmother decided I was old enough to help her run the ferry that crossed the Wabash River between our town and the county seat. The ferry itself was nothing grand: a flat wooden barge with a diesel engine bolted to the stern, capable of carrying two cars and a handful of foot passengers at a time. My grandmother, whom everyone in town called Captain Odile though she had never served in any navy, had operated it for thirty-one years, ever since her husband\'s death left her the only person willing to learn the engine\'s temperamental habits.\n\nI had expected the job to be tedious. Instead, I found myself fascinated by the river\'s moods. On clear mornings it ran the color of weak tea, calm enough that Odile let me steer while she checked the manifest of cars waiting on the near bank. On stormy afternoons, when the water rose brown and fast against the pilings, she took the wheel herself and made me stand beside her, watching how she read the current, how she angled the barge upstream before letting it drift down into the landing on the far side, always compensating, never fighting the river head-on.\n\n"You don\'t beat a river," she told me once, during a particularly rough crossing in late June. "You listen to what it\'s already doing and you find the part of that you can use." I didn\'t understand what she meant then, not really. I nodded because it seemed like the kind of thing you were supposed to nod at, filed the words away, and went back to watching the churn of brown water sliding past the hull.\n\nMost days were quieter. We carried farmers hauling grain to the co-op on the county seat side, and schoolteachers who lived in town but taught in the consolidated school across the river, and occasionally a family of tourists who had gotten lost looking for the state park twelve miles north and ended up, bewildered, at our little crossing instead. Odile knew nearly everyone by name, and she collected their stor