    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Chemistry students conducted two experiments to study how different catalysts affect the decomposition rate of hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) into water and oxygen gas. Reaction rate was measured as the volume of oxygen gas (in milliliters) collected over a fixed 60-second interval after the catalyst was added to a 100 mL sample of 3% hydrogen peroxide solution held at room temperature (22 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested four catalysts, each added in an equal mass of 0.5 grams: manganese dioxide (MnO2), potato extract (containing the enzyme catalase), yeast extract (also containing catalase), and a control condition with no catalyst added. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\nMnO2: 68 mL\nPotato extract: 45 mL\nYeast extract: 52 mL\nNo catalyst (control): 3 mL\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only MnO2 as the catalyst, students varied the temperature of the hydrogen peroxide solution before adding the catalyst, keeping the catalyst mass and solution volume the same as in Experiment 1. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\n10 C: 41 mL\n22 C: 68 mL\n35 C: 89 mL\n50 C: 97 mL\n65 C: 71 mL\n\nStudents noted that at 65 C, the hydrogen peroxide solution appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added, suggesting that heat alone, independent of the catalyst, was beginning to break down the hydrogen peroxide at that temperature.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of the no-catalyst control condition in Experiment 1?',
    options: [
      'To establish the baseline rate of oxygen gas production without any added catalyst, allowing comparison to the catalyzed conditions',
      'To determine the maximum possible reaction rate achievable under any condition',
      'To test whether hydrogen peroxide could be safely stored at room temperature',
      'To measure the effect of solution volume on reaction rate',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The control condition, producing only 3 mL of oxygen gas without any catalyst, provides a baseline against which the effectiveness of MnO2, potato extract, and yeast extract can be meaningfully compared, showing how much each catalyst accelerated the reaction beyond the natural, uncatalyzed rate. The control condition produced the lowest, not the highest, oxygen volume, so it does not represent a maximum achievable rate under any condition. The experiment does not address storage safety of hydrogen peroxide; it measures reaction rate under active experimental conditions. Solution volume is held constant at 100 mL across all conditions in the passage, meaning the control was not designed to test the effect of varying volume. ACT Tip: A no-treatment control condition in a catalyst or treatment-comparison experiment typically establishes a baseline rate, allowing the treatment conditions\' effects to be measured as a change relative to that baseline.',
  ),
