    explanation: 'Table 1 lists Site B, Year 4 temperature as 28.7 C. The value 27.9 C corresponds to Site B, Year 1, not Year 4. The value 29.0 C corresponds to Site B, Year 3, not Year 4. The value 29.8 C corresponds to Site B, Year 5, not Year 4. ACT Tip: Double-check that you are reading the value from the correct year\'s row within a site\'s data before selecting an answer, especially when adjacent years have similar values.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-027',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Chemistry students conducted two experiments to study how different catalysts affect the decomposition rate of hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) into water and oxygen gas. Reaction rate was measured as the volume of oxygen gas (in milliliters) collected over a fixed 60-second interval after the catalyst was added to a 100 mL sample of 3% hydrogen peroxide solution held at room temperature (22 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested four catalysts, each added in an equal mass of 0.5 grams: manganese dioxide (MnO2), potato extract (containing the enzyme catalase), yeast extract (also containing catalase), and a control condition with no catalyst added. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\nMnO2: 68 mL\nPotato extract: 45 mL\nYeast extract: 52 mL\nNo catalyst (control): 3 mL\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only MnO2 as the catalyst, students varied the temperature of the hydrogen peroxide solution before adding the catalyst, keeping the catalyst mass and solution volume the same as in Experiment 1. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\n10 C: 41 mL\n22 C: 68 mL\n35 C: 89 mL\n50 C: 97 mL\n65 C: 71 mL\n\nStudents noted that at 65 C, the hydrogen peroxide solution appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added, suggesting that heat alone, independent of the catalyst, was beginning to break down the hydrogen peroxide at that temperature.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, at which temperature was the greatest volume of oxygen gas collected?',
    options: [
      '35 C',
      '65 C',
      '22 C',
      '50 C',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports 97 mL of oxygen gas collected at 50 C, the highest value among the five temperatures tested (41 mL, 68 mL, 89 mL, 97 mL, and 71 mL). At 22 C, 68 mL was collected, lower than at 50 C. At 35 C, 89 mL was collected, higher than at 22 C but still lower than at 50 C. At 65 C, only 71 mL was collected, notably lower than the peak at 50 C. ACT Tip: When a data set shows values rising and then falling across a range, such as temperature here, identify the single peak value rather than assuming the highest value occurs at the highest or lowest tested point.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-033',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Chemistry students conducted two experiments to study how different catalysts affect the decomposition rate of hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) into water and oxygen gas. Reaction rate was measured as the volume of oxygen gas (in milliliters) collected over a fixed 60-second interval after the catalyst was added to a 100 mL sample of 3% hydrogen peroxide solution held at room temperature (22 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested four catalysts, each added in an equal mass of 0.5 grams: manganese dioxide (MnO2), potato extract (containing the enzyme catalase), yeast extract (also containing catalase), and a control condition with no catalyst added. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\nMnO2: 68 mL\nPotato extract: 45 mL\nYeast extract: 52 mL\nNo catalyst (control): 3 mL\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only MnO2 as the catalyst, students varied the temperature of the hydrogen peroxide solution before adding the catalyst, keeping the catalyst mass and solution volume the same as in Experiment 1. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\n10 C: 41 mL\n22 C: 68 mL\n35 C: 89 mL\n50 C: 97 mL\n65 C: 71 mL\n\nStudents noted that at 65 C, the hydrogen peroxide solution appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added, suggesting that heat alone, independent of the catalyst, was beginning to break down the hydrogen peroxide at that temperature.',
    questionText: 'A student proposes that reaction rate increases indefinitely as temperature increases, with no upper limit. Do the results of Experiment 2 support this proposal?',
    options: [
      'No, because oxygen gas volume increased from 10 C up to 50 C but then decreased at 65 C, indicating a limit rather than an indefinite increase',
      'No, because temperature was not actually varied in Experiment 2',
      'Yes, because the highest temperature tested, 65 C, also produced the highest oxygen gas volume',
      'Yes, because oxygen gas volume increased at every single temperature tested from 10 C to 65 C',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The data in Experiment 2 shows oxygen volume rising from 41 mL at 10 C to a peak of 97 mL at 50 C, but then falling to 71 mL at 65 C, directly contradicting a claim of indefinite increase and instead showing a clear upper limit or optimal point. Oxygen gas volume did not increase at every temperature tested; it decreased between 50 C and 65 C, making the first option factually incorrect based on the data. The highest temperature tested, 65 C, produced 71 mL, which is lower than the 97 mL produced at 50 C, directly contradicting the claim that the highest temperature produced the highest volume. Temperature was explicitly varied across five distinct values in Experiment 2, directly contradicting the claim that it was not varied. ACT Tip: To evaluate a claim of an indefinite or unlimited trend, check the data across its full range, including the highest values tested, rather than assuming the pattern from early values continues unchanged.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-010',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'Based on Figure 1, as ambient noise level increased across the six locations, minimum song frequency:',
    options: [
      'increased',
      'remained constant',
      'decreased',
      'increased and then decreased',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Across all six locations, as noise level rises from 42 dB to 79 dB, minimum song frequency rises correspondingly from 2.1 kHz to 4.4 kHz, showing a consistent increasing trend. The data shows no decrease in frequency at any point as noise increases, ruling out the decreasing trend option. The frequency values change substantially across locations rather than remaining constant, ruling out that option. The trend increases steadily throughout the entire data set with no reversal, ruling out an increase-then-decrease pattern. ACT Tip: To identify an overall trend across multiple data points, check whether the pattern holds consistently across every pair of adjacent values, not just the first and last.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-021',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, how many seedlings were included in each light condition group across the three experiments?',
    options: [
      '21',
      '50',
      '30',
      '10',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that "each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values." The number 21 refers to the number of days the seedlings were grown, not the number of seedlings per group. The numbers 10 and 50 are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: Be careful to distinguish between different numerical details in a passage, such as sample size versus duration, which can be easily confused if read too quickly.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-031',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Chemistry students conducted two experiments to study how different catalysts affect the decomposition rate of hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) into water and oxygen gas. Reaction rate was measured as the volume of oxygen gas (in milliliters) collected over a fixed 60-second interval after the catalyst was added to a 100 mL sample of 3% hydrogen peroxide solution held at room temperature (22 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested four catalysts, each added in an equal mass of 0.5 grams: manganese dioxide (MnO2), potato extract (containing the enzyme catalase), yeast extract (also containing catalase), and a control condition with no catalyst added. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\nMnO2: 68 mL\nPotato extract: 45 mL\nYeast extract: 52 mL\nNo catalyst (control): 3 mL\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only MnO2 as the catalyst, students varied the temperature of the hydrogen peroxide solution before adding the catalyst, keeping the catalyst mass and solution volume the same as in Experiment 1. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\n10 C: 41 mL\n22 C: 68 mL\n35 C: 89 mL\n50 C: 97 mL\n65 C: 71 mL\n\nStudents noted that at 65 C, the hydrogen peroxide solution appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added, suggesting that heat alone, independent of the catalyst, was beginning to break down the hydrogen peroxide at that temperature.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, which of the following statements is best supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'MnO2 was more effective than the two catalase-containing catalysts at room temperature, and its effectiveness further increased with temperature up to a point before declining',
      'The control condition without a catalyst produced more oxygen gas than any catalyzed condition',
      'Temperature has no measurable effect on the rate of hydrogen peroxide decomposition',
      'Catalase-containing catalysts are always more effective than MnO2 at any temperature',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 shows MnO2 (68 mL) outperforming both potato extract (45 mL) and yeast extract (52 mL) at room temperature, and Experiment 2 shows MnO2\'s effectiveness rising further with temperature up to 50 C (97 mL) before declining at 65 C (71 mL), directly supporting this combined statement. Experiment 1 shows the opposite relationship at room temperature, with MnO2 outperforming both catalase-containing catalysts, contradicting the claim that catalase-containing catalysts are always more effective. Experiment 2 directly shows temperature substantially affecting oxygen production, with volumes ranging from 41 mL to 97 mL across different temperatures, directly contradicting the claim of no measurable effect. The control condition produced only 3 mL, far less than any catalyzed condition in Experiment 1, directly contradicting this claim. ACT Tip: When a question asks about a claim supported by two experiments together, verify that each part of the combined statement is separately supported by its corresponding experiment\'s specific data.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-034',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Approximately 252 million years ago, at the end of the Permian period, Earth experienced its most severe mass extinction event, during which an estimated 90 percent of marine species and 70 percent of land vertebrate species disappeared from the fossil record within a geologically short interval. Two scientists offer differing explanations for the primary cause of this extinction event.\n\nScientist 1: Volcanic Cause\nThe primary driver of the end-Permian extinction was a massive episode of volcanic activity centered in what is now Siberia, known as the Siberian Traps. Over a period of roughly one million years, these eruptions released enormous volumes of lava, covering an area comparable in size to the continental United States, along with massive quantities of carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide gas. The carbon dioxide caused severe global warming, raising average ocean temperatures by an estimated 8 to 10 degrees Celsius, while sulfur dioxide combined with atmospheric moisture to produce widespread acid rain, damaging plant life on land and altering ocean chemistry. The warming oceans also held less dissolved oxygen, creating widespread marine dead zones that account for the extremely high rate of marine species loss. This volcanic explanation is supported by the precise geological dating of the Siberian Traps eruptions, which closely matches the timing of the extinction event in multiple independently dated rock sequences around the world, and by chemical signatures in rock layers from this period indicating a massive release of carbon into the atmosphere and oceans consistent with volcanic outgassing on this scale.\n\nScientist 2: Extraterrestrial Impact Cause\nWhile volcanic activity certainly occurred during this period, it cannot fully account for the severity and specific pattern of the end-Permian extinction. The primary cause was instead a large asteroid or comet impact, similar in mechanism to the impact that caused the later extinction of the dinosaurs at the end of the Cretaceous period, though direct physical evidence such as an identified impact crater from this specific event has not yet been conclusively located, likely due to the extreme age of the rock layers involved and the substantial geological changes Earth\'s surface has undergone over 252 million years. An impact of sufficient size would have thrown enormous quantities of dust and debris into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight and causing a sharp, relatively short-term collapse in photosynthesis on land and in the oceans, which would explain the extinction\'s remarkably rapid onset in the geological record, a timescale that seems difficult to reconcile with a volcanic process unfolding over roughly one million years. Additionally, some rock layers from this boundary period contain unusual mineral grains showing signs of shock metamorphism, a type of structural deformation typically associated with the intense pressures of a large impact event rather than with volcanic processes, along with trace amounts of elements that are rare in Earth\'s crust but more common in certain types of asteroids.',
    questionText: 'According to Scientist 1, what geological feature is identified as the primary source of the volcanic activity causing the extinction?',
    options: [
      'The Siberian Traps',
      'The Deccan Traps',
      'The Yellowstone caldera',
      'The Chicxulub crater',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Scientist 1 explicitly identifies "a massive episode of volcanic activity centered in what is now Siberia, known as the Siberian Traps" as the primary driver discussed. The Deccan Traps are a real volcanic feature associated with a different, later extinction event, but they are not mentioned anywhere in this passage. The Chicxulub crater is associated with the extraterrestrial impact that caused the end-Cretaceous extinction, referenced only as a comparison by Scientist 2, not as the feature discussed by Scientist 1. The Yellowstone caldera is not mentioned anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: In a Conflicting Viewpoints passage, carefully attribute each specific named feature or piece of evidence to the correct scientist before answering, since the two viewpoints often reference similarly structured but distinct evidence.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-024',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'A grower wants to maximize both plant height and leaf surface area simultaneously for a commercial lettuce crop. Based on Experiments 1 and 2, which light condition would best achieve this goal?',
    options: [
      'Red/blue mix, since it produced the highest value for both plant height and leaf surface area',
      'White light (control), since it represents a balanced natural light source',
      'Red only, since it produced reasonably high values in both measures',
      'Blue only, since it produced the second-highest leaf surface area',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The red/blue mix produced the highest average height (11.4 cm) in Experiment 1 and the highest average leaf surface area (61 cm^2) in Experiment 2, making it the single condition that best satisfies the goal of maximizing both measures simultaneously. Red only produced the lowest leaf surface area of all four conditions in Experiment 2, making it a poor choice for maximizing that measure despite a moderate height result. Blue only produced the lowest height of all four conditions in Experiment 1, making it a poor choice for maximizing height despite a relatively high leaf area result. White light produced only moderate, non-maximal values in both experiments, making it a less effective choice than the red/blue mix for achieving both goals simultaneously. ACT Tip: When asked to optimize for two different measured outcomes at once, identify the single condition that ranks highest across both relevant data sets, rather than a condition that performs well in only one.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-001',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Marine biologists monitored four coral reef sites over a five-year period, recording the average sea surface temperature (in degrees Celsius) during the warmest month of each year and the percentage of coral colonies at each site showing visible signs of bleaching (a stress response in which corals expel the algae that give them color and nutrients). Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Warmest-Month Sea Surface Temperature and Percent Coral Bleaching by Site and Year\n\nSite A (shallow, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.2 C, Bleaching 4%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.5 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 22%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.1 C, Bleaching 15%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.3 C, Bleaching 41%\n\nSite B (deep, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.9 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.1 C, Bleaching 3%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.0 C, Bleaching 9%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.7 C, Bleaching 7%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 19%\n\nSite C (shallow, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.4 C, Bleaching 5%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 28%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.3 C, Bleaching 18%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.5 C, Bleaching 47%\n\nSite D (deep, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.8 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.0 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 3: Temperature 28.9 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 17%\n\nResearchers also noted that scientists generally consider 29.5 C to be a critical threshold above which bleaching risk increases sharply at reef sites in this region.',
    questionText: 'According to Table 1, what was the percent bleaching at Site C in Year 3?',
    options: [
      '28%',
      '47%',
      '18%',
      '22%',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Table 1 lists Site C, Year 3 as having a bleaching percentage of 28%. The value 18% corresponds to Site C in Year 4, not Year 3. The value 22% corresponds to Site A in Year 3, not Site C. The value 47% corresponds to Site C in Year 5, not Year 3. ACT Tip: When reading a data table with multiple sites and years, trace both the correct row and the correct column carefully before selecting a value, since nearby values for related site-years are common distractors.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-035',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Approximately 252 million years ago, at the end of the Permian period, Earth experienced its most severe mass extinction event, during which an estimated 90 percent of marine species and 70 percent of land vertebrate species disappeared from the fossil record within a geologically short interval. Two scientists offer differing explanations for the primary cause of this extinction event.\n\nScientist 1: Volcanic Cause\nThe primary driver of the end-Permian extinction was a massive episode of volcanic activity centered in what is now Siberia, known as the Siberian Traps. Over a period of roughly one million years, these eruptions released enormous volumes of lava, covering an area comparable in size to the continental United States, along with massive quantities of carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide gas. The carbon dioxide caused severe global warming, raising average ocean temperatures by an estimated 8 to 10 degrees Celsius, while sulfur dioxide combined with atmospheric moisture to produce widespread acid rain, damaging plant life on land and altering ocean chemistry. The warming oceans also held less dissolved oxygen, creating widespread marine dead zones that account for the extremely high rate of marine species loss. This volcanic explanation is supported by the precise geological dating of the Siberian Traps eruptions, which closely matches the timing of the extinction event in multiple independently dated rock sequences around the world, and by chemical signatures in rock layers from this period indicating a massive release of carbon into the atmosphere and oceans consistent with volcanic outgassing on this scale.\n\nScientist 2: Extraterrestrial Impact Cause\nWhile volcanic activity certainly occurred during this period, it cannot fully account for the severity and specific pattern of the end-Permian extinction. The primary cause was instead a large asteroid or comet impact, similar in mechanism to the impact that caused the later extinction of the dinosaurs at the end of the Cretaceous period, though direct physical evidence such as an identified impact crater from this specific event has not yet been conclusively located, likely due to the extreme age of the rock layers involved and the substantial geological changes Earth\'s surface has undergone over 252 million years. An impact of sufficient size would have thrown enormous quantities of dust and debris into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight and causing a sharp, relatively short-term collapse in photosynthesis on land and in the oceans, which would explain the extinction\'s remarkably rapid onset in the geological record, a timescale that seems difficult to reconcile with a volcanic process unfolding over roughly one million years. Additionally, some rock layers from this boundary period contain unusual mineral grains showing signs of shock metamorphism, a type of structural deformation typically associated with the intense pressures of a large impact event rather than with volcanic processes, along with trace amounts of elements that are rare in Earth\'s crust but more common in certain types of asteroids.',
    questionText: 'According to Scientist 2, what specific piece of physical evidence directly supporting an impact event has NOT yet been found?',
    options: [
      'Evidence of a sharp decline in photosynthesis',
      'Trace amounts of elements rare in Earth\'s crust',
      'Shock metamorphism in mineral grains',
      'A conclusively identified impact crater from this specific event',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Scientist 2 explicitly states that "direct physical evidence such as an identified impact crater from this specific event has not yet been conclusively located." Shock metamorphism in mineral grains is presented as evidence that has been found and that supports the impact hypothesis, not as missing evidence. Trace amounts of rare elements are also presented as evidence that has been found in the rock layers, not as missing evidence. A sharp decline in photosynthesis is presented as an inferred consequence explained by the impact hypothesis, not as a specific type of missing physical evidence being discussed. ACT Tip: In a Conflicting Viewpoints passage, distinguish carefully between evidence a scientist presents as already found and supporting their view, and evidence the scientist explicitly acknowledges is still missing or unconfirmed.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-039',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Approximately 252 million years ago, at the end of the Permian period, Earth experienced its most severe mass extinction event, during which an estimated 90 percent of marine species and 70 percent of land vertebrate species disappeared from the fossil record within a geologically short interval. Two scientists offer differing explanations for the primary cause of this extinction event.\n\nScientist 1: Volcanic Cause\nThe primary driver of the end-Permian extinction was a massive episode of volcanic activity centered in what is now Siberia, known as the Siberian Traps. Over a period of roughly one million years, these eruptions released enormous volumes of lava, covering an area comparable in size to the continental United States, along with massive quantities of carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide gas. The carbon dioxide caused severe global warming, raising average ocean temperatures by an estimated 8 to 10 degrees Celsius, while sulfur dioxide combined with atmospheric moisture to produce widespread acid rain, damaging plant life on land and altering ocean chemistry. The warming oceans also held less dissolved oxygen, creating widespread marine dead zones that account for the extremely high rate of marine species loss. This volcanic explanation is supported by the precise geological dating of the Siberian Traps eruptions, which closely matches the timing of the extinction event in multiple independently dated rock sequences around the world, and by chemical signatures in rock layers from this period indicating a massive release of carbon into the atmosphere and oceans consistent with volcanic outgassing on this scale.\n\nScientist 2: Extraterrestrial Impact Cause\nWhile volcanic activity certainly occurred during this period, it cannot fully account for the severity and specific pattern of the end-Permian extinction. The primary cause was instead a large asteroid or comet impact, similar in mechanism to the impact that caused the later extinction of the dinosaurs at the end of the Cretaceous period, though direct physical evidence such as an identified impact crater from this specific event has not yet been conclusively located, likely due to the extreme age of the rock layers involved and the substantial geological changes Earth\'s surface has undergone over 252 million years. An impact of sufficient size would have thrown enormous quantities of dust and debris into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight and causing a sharp, relatively short-term collapse in photosynthesis on land and in the oceans, which would explain the extinction\'s remarkably rapid onset in the geological record, a timescale that seems difficult to reconcile with a volcanic process unfolding over roughly one million years. Additionally, some rock layers from this boundary period contain unusual mineral grains showing signs of shock metamorphism, a type of structural deformation typically associated with the intense pressures of a large impact event rather than with volcanic processes, along with trace amounts of elements that are rare in Earth\'s crust but more common in certain types of asteroids.',
    questionText: 'How does Scientist 2 respond to the fact that no impact crater from this event has been conclusively identified?',
    options: [
      'By suggesting that the extreme age of the relevant rock layers and substantial geological changes over 252 million years may explain why a crater has not yet been located',
      'By arguing that the crater was likely destroyed during the extinction event itself',
      'By conceding that the absence of a crater disproves the impact hypothesis entirely',
      'By claiming that impact craters are never detectable after more than 100 million years under any circumstances',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Scientist 2 explicitly attributes the missing crater to "the extreme age of the rock layers involved and the substantial geological changes Earth\'s surface has undergone over 252 million years," offering this as an explanation for the gap rather than treating it as a fatal flaw in the hypothesis. Scientist 2 does not concede that the missing crater disproves the hypothesis; instead, the scientist offers a specific explanation for why it has not yet been found while still maintaining the impact explanation. The passage does not state or suggest that the crater was destroyed specifically during the extinction event itself; the explanation given relates to age and general geological change, not a specific destructive process tied to the extinction. Scientist 2 does not make the sweeping claim that craters are never detectable after 100 million years under any circumstances; the reasoning given is specific to this event\'s age and geological history, not a universal claim about crater detectability. ACT Tip: When a scientist acknowledges a gap or weakness in their own evidence, look for how they specifically explain or account for that gap rather than assuming they either ignore it or treat it as disproving their view entirely.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-014',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'A critic argues that the pattern in Figure 1 could be explained entirely by different bird populations at each location having naturally different average song frequencies, unrelated to noise level. Which piece of evidence from the passage most directly weakens this alternative explanation?',
    options: [
      'The fact that noise levels were measured in decibels rather than another unit',
      'The fact that six different locations were studied rather than just one',
      'The fact that Location 6 had the highest noise level of all six locations studied',
      'The nighttime road closure data, which showed a frequency change at the same location and presumably the same bird population when noise level temporarily changed',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Because the road closure data involves the same location and presumably the same bird population experiencing a temporary noise reduction, the accompanying frequency decrease cannot be explained by different populations having different fixed frequencies, since no population change occurred, directly weakening the critic\'s alternative explanation. Studying six different locations alone does not resolve the critic\'s concern, since the critic\'s explanation specifically proposes that different locations have different bird populations with different fixed frequencies. The unit of measurement used for noise level has no bearing on whether the observed pattern reflects population differences or a genuine response to noise. Location 6 having the highest noise level is consistent with both the original hypothesis and the critic\'s alternative explanation, so it does not help distinguish between them. ACT Tip: To weaken an alternative explanation involving a hidden confounding variable, such as different populations, look for evidence from a scenario where that confounding variable did not change, such as the same location and population being measured under different conditions.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-002',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Marine biologists monitored four coral reef sites over a five-year period, recording the average sea surface temperature (in degrees Celsius) during the warmest month of each year and the percentage of coral colonies at each site showing visible signs of bleaching (a stress response in which corals expel the algae that give them color and nutrients). Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Warmest-Month Sea Surface Temperature and Percent Coral Bleaching by Site and Year\n\nSite A (shallow, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.2 C, Bleaching 4%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.5 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 22%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.1 C, Bleaching 15%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.3 C, Bleaching 41%\n\nSite B (deep, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.9 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.1 C, Bleaching 3%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.0 C, Bleaching 9%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.7 C, Bleaching 7%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 19%\n\nSite C (shallow, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.4 C, Bleaching 5%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 28%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.3 C, Bleaching 18%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.5 C, Bleaching 47%\n\nSite D (deep, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.8 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.0 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 3: Temperature 28.9 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 17%\n\nResearchers also noted that scientists generally consider 29.5 C to be a critical threshold above which bleaching risk increases sharply at reef sites in this region.',
    questionText: 'Based on Table 1, which site consistently showed the lowest percent bleaching across all five years?',
    options: [
      'Site A',
      'Site C',
      'Site B',
      'Site D',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Comparing bleaching values across all five years for each site, Site D\'s values (2%, 2%, 8%, 6%, 17%) are lower than or equal to the corresponding values at every other site in every year, making it consistently the lowest. Site A shows notably higher bleaching in Years 3 and 5 (22% and 41%) compared to Site D\'s 8% and 17% in those same years. Site B\'s values are generally higher than Site D\'s in every year, such as 9% versus 8% in Year 3 and 19% versus 17% in Year 5. Site C shows the highest bleaching values of all four sites in most years, including 28% and 47% in Years 3 and 5, far exceeding Site D. ACT Tip: To identify a consistent pattern across multiple categories in a table, compare the same year across all groups systematically rather than looking at only one or two years.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-032',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Chemistry students conducted two experiments to study how different catalysts affect the decomposition rate of hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) into water and oxygen gas. Reaction rate was measured as the volume of oxygen gas (in milliliters) collected over a fixed 60-second interval after the catalyst was added to a 100 mL sample of 3% hydrogen peroxide solution held at room temperature (22 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested four catalysts, each added in an equal mass of 0.5 grams: manganese dioxide (MnO2), potato extract (containing the enzyme catalase), yeast extract (also containing catalase), and a control condition with no catalyst added. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\nMnO2: 68 mL\nPotato extract: 45 mL\nYeast extract: 52 mL\nNo catalyst (control): 3 mL\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only MnO2 as the catalyst, students varied the temperature of the hydrogen peroxide solution before adding the catalyst, keeping the catalyst mass and solution volume the same as in Experiment 1. Oxygen gas collected after 60 seconds:\n10 C: 41 mL\n22 C: 68 mL\n35 C: 89 mL\n50 C: 97 mL\n65 C: 71 mL\n\nStudents noted that at 65 C, the hydrogen peroxide solution appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added, suggesting that heat alone, independent of the catalyst, was beginning to break down the hydrogen peroxide at that temperature.',
    questionText: 'Why did the students hold the hydrogen peroxide concentration constant at 3% across all trials in both experiments?',
    options: [
      'To ensure the reaction would take exactly 60 seconds to complete in every trial',
      'Because 3% is the maximum concentration of hydrogen peroxide that can decompose into water and oxygen',
      'Because higher concentrations of hydrogen peroxide are not available for purchase',
      'To isolate the effects of catalyst type and temperature on reaction rate without the added variable of differing hydrogen peroxide concentrations',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'By keeping hydrogen peroxide concentration constant across all trials, the students ensured that any differences in oxygen gas produced could be attributed specifically to the variable being tested, catalyst type in Experiment 1 or temperature in Experiment 2, rather than to differences in the starting concentration of reactant. The passage does not state or imply that 3% represents any kind of maximum concentration capable of decomposing; this is an unsupported claim about the chemistry involved. The passage gives no information about the availability of higher-concentration hydrogen peroxide for purchase. The 60-second interval is described as a fixed measurement window for collecting gas, not a claim that the reaction fully completes within that time in every trial. ACT Tip: When a variable is described as being "held constant" across an experiment\'s trials, its purpose is almost always to isolate the effect of the variable that is deliberately changed.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-003',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Marine biologists monitored four coral reef sites over a five-year period, recording the average sea surface temperature (in degrees Celsius) during the warmest month of each year and the percentage of coral colonies at each site showing visible signs of bleaching (a stress response in which corals expel the algae that give them color and nutrients). Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Warmest-Month Sea Surface Temperature and Percent Coral Bleaching by Site and Year\n\nSite A (shallow, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.2 C, Bleaching 4%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.5 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 22%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.1 C, Bleaching 15%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.3 C, Bleaching 41%\n\nSite B (deep, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.9 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.1 C, Bleaching 3%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.0 C, Bleaching 9%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.7 C, Bleaching 7%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 19%\n\nSite C (shallow, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.4 C, Bleaching 5%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 28%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.3 C, Bleaching 18%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.5 C, Bleaching 47%\n\nSite D (deep, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.8 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.0 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 3: Temperature 28.9 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 17%\n\nResearchers also noted that scientists generally consider 29.5 C to be a critical threshold above which bleaching risk increases sharply at reef sites in this region.',
    questionText: 'According to Table 1, in which year did Site A\'s warmest-month sea surface temperature first exceed the 29.5 C threshold mentioned in the passage?',
    options: [
      'Year 3',
      'Year 4',
      'Year 2',
      'Year 5',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Site A\'s temperatures were 28.2 C, 28.5 C, 29.6 C, 29.1 C, and 30.3 C for Years 1 through 5, respectively. Years 1 and 2 remain below the 29.5 C threshold. Year 3\'s temperature of 29.6 C is the first value to exceed 29.5 C, making Year 3 the correct answer, even though Year 4 dips back below the threshold to 29.1 C before Year 5 exceeds it again. ACT Tip: When a question asks for the first time a threshold is crossed, check every year in sequence rather than skipping ahead to a later year with an even higher value, since an earlier, smaller crossing is easy to overlook.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-015',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, which location had an ambient noise level closest to 60 dB?',
    options: [
      'Location 5',
      'Location 4',
      'Location 3',
      'Location 2',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Location 3 has a noise level of 58 dB, which is closer to 60 dB than any other location\'s value. Location 2\'s noise level of 51 dB is 9 dB away from 60 dB, farther than Location 3\'s 2 dB difference. Location 4\'s noise level of 66 dB is 6 dB away from 60 dB, farther than Location 3\'s 2 dB difference. Location 5\'s noise level of 72 dB is 12 dB away from 60 dB, considerably farther than Location 3\'s 2 dB difference. ACT Tip: When asked to find the value closest to a target number, calculate the absolute difference between the target and each candidate value before selecting the smallest difference.',
