  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-016',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, which light condition produced the greatest average plant height after 21 days?',
    options: [
      'White light (control)',
      'Blue only',
      'Red only',
      'Red/blue mix',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 reports the red/blue mix condition produced an average height of 11.4 cm, the highest value among the four conditions listed (8.2 cm, 6.1 cm, 11.4 cm, and 9.8 cm). Red only produced a lower height of 8.2 cm. Blue only produced the lowest height of all four conditions at 6.1 cm. White light, the control, produced 9.8 cm, lower than the red/blue mix. ACT Tip: When a question asks for the greatest or least value among several listed conditions, scan all values in the relevant experiment before selecting an answer.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-018',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, which light condition produced the greatest average leaf surface area after 21 days?',
    options: [
      'Red only',
      'White light (control)',
      'Blue only',
      'Red/blue mix',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports the red/blue mix produced 61 cm^2, the highest value among the four conditions (42 cm^2, 58 cm^2, 61 cm^2, and 50 cm^2). Red only produced the lowest leaf area of all four conditions at 42 cm^2. Blue only produced 58 cm^2, higher than red only and white light but still lower than the red/blue mix. White light produced 50 cm^2, lower than the red/blue mix. ACT Tip: Even when two experiments measure different variables, such as height and leaf area, check each experiment\'s data independently rather than assuming the same condition will rank highest in both.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-017',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of including a white light (control) condition in Experiments 1 and 2?',
    options: [
      'To provide a baseline representing typical broad-spectrum light against which the effects of specific wavelength combinations could be compared',
      'To measure the effect of temperature on seedling growth independent of light',
      'To determine the maximum possible height lettuce seedlings could reach under any condition',
      'To test whether seedlings could survive without any light at all',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The white light condition uses "a broad mix of all visible wavelengths," functioning as a standard, general-purpose lighting baseline against which the more targeted red-only, blue-only, and red/blue mix conditions can be meaningfully compared. The white light condition did not necessarily produce the maximum height in either experiment; the red/blue mix outperformed it in Experiment 1, showing the control was not intended to represent an upper limit. All light conditions in the passage involve light exposure; no condition involves growing seedlings in complete darkness, so testing survival without light is not addressed. Temperature is explicitly described as held constant across all groups, meaning the white light control was not designed to isolate temperature effects. ACT Tip: A control condition in an experiment comparing several treatment variations typically represents a standard or typical baseline condition, not necessarily the best or most extreme outcome.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-009',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, what was the minimum song frequency recorded at Location 4?',
    options: [
      '3.9 kHz',
      '2.9 kHz',
      '3.5 kHz',
      '4.4 kHz',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Figure 1 lists Location 4 (near a four-lane road) with a minimum song frequency of 3.5 kHz. The value 2.9 kHz corresponds to Location 3, not Location 4. The value 3.9 kHz corresponds to Location 5, not Location 4. The value 4.4 kHz corresponds to Location 6, not Location 4. ACT Tip: When several locations have closely spaced values, trace the exact location number stated in the question to the matching data point before selecting an answer.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-iod-013',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'Based on the pattern shown in Figure 1, if a seventh location had an ambient noise level of approximately 85 dB, the minimum song frequency at that location would most likely be closest to which of the following?',
    options: [
      '5.8 kHz',
      '4.4 kHz',
      '4.9 kHz',
      '3.8 kHz',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The data shows a consistent increasing trend, with frequency rising roughly 0.5 kHz for every 7 to 8 dB increase in noise level between Locations 5 and 6 (72 dB to 79 dB corresponds to 3.9 kHz to 4.4 kHz). Extending this pattern from Location 6 (79 dB, 4.4 kHz) to approximately 85 dB, an increase of about 6 dB, would predict a frequency increase of roughly 0.4 to 0.5 kHz, landing close to 4.9 kHz. A value of 3.8 kHz is lower than Location 6\'s already-recorded 4.4 kHz, which is inconsistent with a further noise increase producing a lower frequency. A value of 4.4 kHz would represent no change at all from Location 6 despite a further noise increase, which is inconsistent with the established trend. A value of 5.8 kHz represents a larger jump than the trend established between any two adjacent locations in the data set, making it a less reasonable extrapolation than 4.9 kHz. ACT Tip: When extrapolating beyond the given data, apply the rate of change observed between the nearest two existing data points rather than assuming a much larger or smaller jump.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-sci-023',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, which light condition produced the lowest average plant height but not the lowest average leaf surface area?',
    options: [
      'Blue only',
      'White light (control)',
      'Red/blue mix',
      'Red only',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Blue only produced the lowest height in Experiment 1 (6.1 cm, lower than 8.2, 11.4, and 9.8 cm), but in Experiment 2 it produced 58 cm^2 of leaf area, which is higher than red only\'s 42 cm^2, meaning blue only did not have the lowest leaf area. Red only had the lowest leaf area in Experiment 2 (42 cm^2) but not the lowest height in Experiment 1, since blue only\'s height of 6.1 cm was lower than red only\'s 8.2 cm, making red only an incorrect match for this specific combination of conditions. The red/blue mix produced the highest values in both experiments, not the lowest in either. White light produced middle-range values in both experiments, neither the lowest height nor the lowest leaf area. ACT Tip: When a question asks about a specific combination of results across two experiments, check each experiment\'s rankings separately before identifying which single condition matches both stated criteria.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
