  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-019',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'A team of botanists conducted three experiments to study how different wavelengths of light affect the growth of lettuce seedlings grown indoors under controlled conditions. In each experiment, seedlings of the same age and initial size were divided into groups and grown for 21 days under LED lights emitting specific wavelength ranges, with all other conditions (temperature, water, soil nutrients, light duration of 14 hours per day) held constant across groups.\n\nExperiment 1\nSeedlings were grown under four light conditions: red light only (630-660 nm), blue light only (450-470 nm), a 50/50 mix of red and blue light, and white light (a broad mix of all visible wavelengths) as a control. After 21 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nRed only: 8.2 cm\nBlue only: 6.1 cm\nRed/blue mix: 11.4 cm\nWhite light (control): 9.8 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four light conditions, researchers measured average leaf surface area, in square centimeters, after 21 days.\nRed only: 42 cm^2\nBlue only: 58 cm^2\nRed/blue mix: 61 cm^2\nWhite light (control): 50 cm^2\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of seedlings under the red/blue mix condition, varying only the ratio of red to blue light, to determine whether an optimal ratio exists. They measured average plant height after 21 days.\n25% red / 75% blue: 9.5 cm\n50% red / 50% blue: 11.4 cm\n75% red / 25% blue: 10.6 cm\n90% red / 10% blue: 8.9 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, seedlings were randomly assigned to light conditions, and each condition included 30 seedlings whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiment 3, a researcher concludes that increasing the proportion of red light beyond 50% always improves plant height. Is this conclusion supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'No, because average height decreased when the red proportion increased from 50% to 75% and decreased further from 75% to 90%',
      'No, because leaf surface area was not measured in Experiment 3',
      'Yes, because red light alone in Experiment 1 produced a greater height than blue light alone',
      'Yes, because the 90% red / 10% blue condition produced the second-highest average height among the four ratios tested',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Experiment 3\'s data shows height peaking at the 50% red / 50% blue ratio (11.4 cm) and then declining at both 75% red (10.6 cm) and 90% red (8.9 cm), directly contradicting the claim that increasing red proportion beyond 50% always improves height. The 90% red / 10% blue condition actually produced the lowest height of all four ratios tested in Experiment 3, not the second-highest, making this option factually incorrect based on the data. The comparison between red-only and blue-only conditions from Experiment 1 does not address the specific claim about increasing red proportion beyond 50% within a mixed-light condition, which is what Experiment 3 specifically tests. While leaf surface area was indeed not measured in Experiment 3, this is not the most direct reason the conclusion is unsupported; the height data itself, which was measured, directly contradicts the claim. ACT Tip: To evaluate a claim about a trend continuing indefinitely, check whether the data shows the trend reversing or plateauing at any point, not just whether it holds between the first two data points.',
  ),
