of the dinosaurs at the end of the Cretaceous period, though direct physical evidence such as an identified impact crater from this specific event has not yet been conclusively located, likely due to the extreme age of the rock layers involved and the substantial geological changes Earth\'s surface has undergone over 252 million years. An impact of sufficient size would have thrown enormous quantities of dust and debris into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight and causing a sharp, relatively short-term collapse in photosynthesis on land and in the oceans, which would explain the extinction\'s remarkably rapid onset in the geological record, a timescale that seems difficult to reconcile with a volcanic process unfolding over roughly one million years. Additionally, some rock layers from this boundary period contain unusual mineral grains showing signs of shock metamorphism, a type of structural deformation typically associated with the intense pressures of a large impact event rather than with volcanic processes, along with trace amounts of elements that are rare in Earth\'s crust but more common in certain types of asteroids.',
