  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-011',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'The nighttime road closure data described in the passage is best interpreted as evidence that:',
    options: [
      'the songbird species cannot sing at all when noise levels fall below 40 dB',
      'the songbird species sings at a fixed minimum frequency regardless of ambient noise level',
      'nighttime and daytime noise levels are always identical at every location',
      'the songbird\'s minimum song frequency tends to decrease when ambient noise level decreases, supporting the idea that noise level influences song frequency rather than location alone',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'When noise dropped from the park\'s typical 42 dB to 38 dB during the road closure, minimum song frequency also dropped slightly, from 2.1 kHz to 2.0 kHz, suggesting the bird\'s frequency responds to actual noise level rather than being fixed by location alone, since the location itself did not change. The data shows frequency did change slightly with the noise reduction, directly contradicting the claim that frequency remains fixed regardless of noise level. The passage states the bird was recorded singing during the closure, directly contradicting the claim that it cannot sing below 40 dB. The passage describes the road closure as producing an unusually low, non-typical noise level specifically because it was rare, directly contradicting the idea that nighttime and daytime levels are always identical. ACT Tip: A natural experiment, such as a temporary noise reduction at an otherwise constant location, can help isolate whether a variable like noise level, rather than location itself, is driving an observed pattern.',
  ),
