    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Marine biologists monitored four coral reef sites over a five-year period, recording the average sea surface temperature (in degrees Celsius) during the warmest month of each year and the percentage of coral colonies at each site showing visible signs of bleaching (a stress response in which corals expel the algae that give them color and nutrients). Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Warmest-Month Sea Surface Temperature and Percent Coral Bleaching by Site and Year\n\nSite A (shallow, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.2 C, Bleaching 4%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.5 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 22%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.1 C, Bleaching 15%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.3 C, Bleaching 41%\n\nSite B (deep, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.9 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.1 C, Bleaching 3%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.0 C, Bleaching 9%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.7 C, Bleaching 7%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 19%\n\nSite C (shallow, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.4 C, Bleaching 5%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 28%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.3 C, Bleaching 18%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.5 C, Bleaching 47%\n\nSite D (deep, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.8 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.0 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 3: Temperature 28.9 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 17%\n\nResearchers also noted that scientists generally consider 29.5 C to be a critical threshold above which bleaching risk increases sharply at reef sites in this region.',
    questionText: 'Based on Table 1, which of the following statements is best supported by a comparison between Site A and Site B?',
